1 NOTE Michelle Aucoin is the ancestral matriarch of the Acadian Boudrot family.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Michelle was born in France around 1621.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Around 1641, Michelle married Michel Boudrot.[1] According to depositions of her descendants, Michelle came to Acadia with her husband Michel.[2] The actual date and place of the marriage is uncertain as Michel is thought to have been in Acadie i
2 CONC n 1639[3], so they would have been married before that if they were married in France and came together. Between about 1642 and 1666, she gave birth to 11 children in Acadie: Françoise, Jeanne, Charles, Marguerite, Marie, Jean, Abraham, Michel, Oli
2 CONC vier, Claude and François.[1]
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 1671 they were counted on the Acadian Census at Port-Royal: Michel BOUDROT, 71, wife Michelle AUCOIN 53; Children (3 married): Francoise 29, Jeanne 25, Marguerite 20; (these not married): Charles 22, Marie 18, Jehan 16, Abraham 14, Michel 12, Ol
2 CONC ivier 10, Claude 8, Francois 5; cattle 5, sheep 12.[4]
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 1678 they were counted on the Acadian Census at Port-Royal: Michel Broudrot and Michelle Aucoin; 12 acres; 10 cattle; 3 guns; 5 boys: 22, 1656; 20, 1658; 18, 1660; 16, 1662; 12, 1666. Michel Boudrot at the brook of brother Joseph Tirat at the so
2 CONC utheast at Maitre Abraham to the River Au Seigneur 2 Leagues.[5]
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 1686, at Port Royal, Michelle AUCOIN, aged 65, was living with her husband, Michel BOUDROT, Lieutenant General of the jurisdiction of Port Royal, aged 85 and their two sons: Michel, aged 26, and Francois, aged 20. The family owned 3 guns and wa
2 CONC s living on 20 arpents of cultivable land with 16 cattle, 17 sheep, and 6 hogs. Their land and animal holdings had significantly increased in the past 8 years.[6]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Some time after this and before 1693, Michel died and Michelle had to carry on.
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 1693, at Port Royal, Michelle AUCOIN, aged 78, widow of Michel BOUDROT, was living with her son, Francois BOUDROT, aged 28, and Francois' wife, Marguerite [sic] BELLINEAU [sic], aged 16. The family owned 1 gun and was living on 25 arpents of cul
2 CONC tivable land with 20 cattle, 18 sheep, and 12 pigs.[7]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Michele Aucoin, wife of Michel Boudrot, died on December 17, 1706, aged more than 85 years at Port-Royal (later renamed Annapolis Royal, Nova Scotia) and was buried there the next day.[8][9]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Michelle Aucoin's passed mtDNA Haplogroup H to her children. She is the Earliest Known Ancestor (EKA) of two FTDNA testers who have taken a Full Sequence mtDNA test, both of whom are reporting Haplogroup H.
