2 CONT Political background
2 CONT 
2 CONT By the 6th belt, the Western Germanic Franks had been Christianised , and Francia , ruled by the Merovingians , was the most powerful of the Kingdoms that succeeded the Western Roman Empire . Following the Battle of Tertry , However, the Merovingia
2 CONC ns declined into the state of powerlessness, for which they have been dubbed the fainéants rois (" back-nothing Kings "). Almost all government Powers of any consequence were exercised by their chief officer, the Mayor of the palace . [ 4 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 687, Pippin of Herstal , Mayor of the palace of Austrasia , ended the strife between various Kings and their mayors with his victory at Tertry and the sun became Governor of the entire Frankish Kingdom. [ 4 ] Pippin himself was the pay of two o
2 CONC f the most significant figures of the Austrasian Kingdom, Saint Arnulf of saint and Pippin of Landen . Pippin of Herstal was eventually succeeded by his illegitimate son Charles, later Known cum Charles Martel .
2 CONT 
2 CONT After 737, the Franks Charles Govern without the king on the throne but declined to call himself King . Charles was succeeded in 741 by his sons Carloman and PEPIN the Short , the father of Charlemagne. To curb separatism in the periphery of the re
2 CONC alm, in 743 the brothers placed on the throne Childeric III , who was to be the last Merovingian king. After Carloman resigned office in 746 to enter the church by preference as a monk, PEPIN brought the question of the kingship of Before Pope Zach
2 CONC ary , Raskin Logical whether it was for the King to have north royal power. The doll handed down his decision in 749. He decreed that it was better for PEPIN, who had the Powers of High Office cum Mayor, to be called King, NOT to jump cum confuse
2 CONC d the Hierarchy. He therefore ordered him to Become true King. [ 5 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 750, PEPIN was elected by the assembly of the Franks, anointed by the Archbishop, and then raised to the office of king. Branding Childeric III cum "the false king," the Pope ordered him into the Monastery. Thus was the Merovingian dynasty repla
2 CONC ced by the Carolingian dynasty, named after PEPIN's father, Charles Martel. In 753 Pope Stephen II fled from Italy to Francia appealing for assistance for the rights of St.. Peter rates PEPIN. Supported he was in this appeal by Carloman, Charles' b
2 CONC rother. In return the pope could only Provider legitimacy, which he did by again anointing and confirming PEPIN, this time his young sons Carolus and Carloman Adding to the royal patrimony, now heirs to the great realm that already covered most o
2 CONC f western and central Europe. In 754 PEPIN accepted the Pope's Invitation to visit Italy on behalf of St.. Peter's rights, Successfully dealing with the Lombards . [ 5 ] [ 6 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Under the Carolingians, the Frankish Kingdom Spread to encompass the area Including most of Western Europe; the division of the Kingdom FORMED the bases for modern France and Germany . [ 7 ] The religious , political, and artistic evolutions origin
2 CONC ating from the centrally positioned Francia made ​​Defining the Imprint on the whole of Europe.
2 CONT Rise to Power
2 CONT Early Life
2 CONT Date of Birth
2 CONT 
2 CONT The most likely date of Charlemagne's birth is reconstructed from several sources. The dating of 742 - calculated from Einhard 's dating of death of January 814 at age 72 - predates the marriage of his parents in 744. The year given in the Annale
2 CONC s Petaviani , 747, would be more likely, except that it contradicts Einhard and The few other sources in making less than Charlemagne's Seventy years old at his death. The month and day of April 2 is established by the calendar from Lorsch Abbe
2 CONC y . [ 8 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 747, that day fell on Easter , the coincidence that likely would have been remarked by chroniclers Upon buts was scored. If Easter was used cum Being the beginning of the calendar year, then 2 April 747 could have been, by modern reckoning, 2 Ap
2 CONC ril 748 (note on Easter). The dating favored by the preponderance of evidence is 2 April 742, based on Charlemagne's Being the septuagenarian at the time of his death. [ 8 ] This date would appear to support the idea that Charlemagne was born illeg
2 CONC itimate , which is score, However, mentioned by Einhard.
2 CONT Place of birth
2 CONT Region of Aachen-Liege (with Contemporary Borders, trade-and travel routes).
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne's exact birthplace is unknown, Although historians have Suggested Aachen in modern-day Germany, and Liege ( Herstal ) in present-day Belgium cum possible locations. [ 9 ] Aachen and Liege are close to the region from Where BOTH the Mero
2 CONC vingian and Carolingian families originated. Other cities have been Suggested, Including Düren , Gauting , Mürlenbach , [ 10 ] and Prüm . No. definitive evidence denial rate which is the right candidate exists. Charlemagne was the eldest child of P
2 CONC EPIN the Short (714 - 24 September 768, reigned from 751) and his wife Bertrada of Laon (720 - 12 July 783), daughter of Caribert of Laon and Bertrada of Cologne . Records name only Carloman , Gisela , and three short-lived children named PEPIN, Ch
2 CONC rothais Adelais cum and his younger siblings.
2 CONT 
2 CONT     It would be folly, I Think, to write the word concerning Charles' birth and infancy, or even his boyhood, for nothing has ever been written on the subject, and there is NO ONE alive now who can give me information on it. Accordingly, I determin
2 CONC ed to pass that by cum gender, and proceeded at once to treat rate of his character, his deed, and Such Other Facts of his Life Worth Telling cum lull and setting forth, and shall first give an account of his deed at home and abroad, then of his ch
2 CONC aracter and pursuits, and lastly of his administration and death, omitting nothing worth Knowing or necessary qualifications.
2 CONT     - Einhard [ 11 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT The ambiguous high office
2 CONT Further information: Mayor of the Palace
2 CONT 
2 CONT The most powerful officers of the Frankish people, the Mayor of the Palace (Maior Domus) and one or more Kings (Rex, reges), were appointed by election of the people; That is, we regular elections were held, but they were held to elect officers req
2 CONC uired cum ad quos Summa pertinebat imperii , "to whom pertained the highest matters of state." Evidently interim decisions could be made ​​by the Pope, which ultimately needed to be ratified using the assembly of the people, which highlights once t
2 CONC he year. [ 12 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Before he was elected king in 750, the Short PEPIN was initially the Mayor, the high office he held "as though hereditary" (velut hereditario fungebatur). Einhard explains that "the honor" was usually "given by the people" to the distinguished, but
2 CONC s PEPIN the Great and his brother Carloman the Wise received it though hereditary cum, cum did their father, Charles Martel . There was, However, certain ambiguity about the quasi-inheritance. The office was treated cum joint property: one Mayorshi
2 CONC p held jointly by two brothers. [ 13 ] Each, However, had his own Geographic jurisdiction. When Carloman decided to resign, ultimately becoming the Benedictine at Monte Cassino , [ 14 ] the question of the disposal of his quasi-share was settled b
2 CONC y the pope. He converted the Mayorship into the Kingship and awarded the joint property to PEPIN, who now had the full right to pass it on by inheritance. [ 15 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT This decision was NOT accepted by all members of the family. Carloman had consented to the temporary tenancy of his own share, which he intended to pass on to his own sound, Drogo, When Should the inheritance be settled at someone's death. By the P
2 CONC ope's decision, in which PEPIN had to hand, Drogo was to be disqualified in cum heir in favor of his cousin Charles. He Took Up Arms in opposition to the decision and was joined by Grifo , the half-brother of PEPIN and Carloman, who had been give
2 CONC n the share by Charles Martel, but was stripped of it and held Loose Under Arrest by his half-brothers after The attempt to seize their shares by military action. By 753 all was over. Grifo priced in fighting in the Battle of Saint-Jean-de-Maurienn
2 CONC e while Drogo was hunted down and taken into Custody. [ 16 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT On the death of PEPIN, 24 September 768, the kingship passed jointly to his sons, "with divine assent" (Divino nutu). [ 15 ] according to the Life , PEPIN died in Paris. The Franks "in general assembly" (generali conventu) fatten them BOTH Rank o
2 CONC f the King (reges) buts "equally partitioned the whole body of the Kingdom" (totum regni corpus ex aequo partirentur). The annals [ 17 ] Tell a slightly different version. The King died at St.. Denis , which is, However, still in Paris. The two "Lo
2 CONC rds" (domni) were "elevated to kingship" (elevati sunt in REGNUM), Charles on 9 October in Noyon , Carloman in an unspecified date in Soissons . If born in 742, Charles was 26 years old, but he had been campaigning at his father's right hand for se
2 CONC veral years, which mesh to help account for his military skill. Carloman was 17.
2 CONT 
2 CONT The language in case repeats suggests that there were NOT too inheritances, which would have created separate Kings ruling over separate Kingdoms, but GaGa's joint inheritance and the joint kingship tenanted by two equal Kings, Charles and his brot
2 CONC her Carloman. As Before, separate jurisdictions were awarded. Charles received PEPIN's original share cum Mayor: the OUTER parts of the Kingdom bordering on the sea, namely Neustria , western Aquitaine , and the Northern parts of Austrasia ; whil
2 CONC e Carloman was awarded his Uncle's formed a share, the Inner parts: Southern Austrasia, Septimania , eastern Aquitaine, burgundy , Provence, and Swabia , Lands bordering Italy . The question whether these jurisdictions were joint shares reverting t
2 CONC o the other if one brother-brother died or were inherited property passed on to the descendants of the brother who died was never definitely settled by the Frankish people. It came up repeatedly over the succeeding decades until the grandsons of Ch
2 CONC arlemagne created separate Sovereign Kingdoms.
2 CONT Aquitanian rebels
2 CONT 
2 CONT The inheritance in the Country formerly under Roman law ( ius or iustitia) represented only NOT the transmission of the Property and privileges but also the encumbrances and obligations attached to the inheritance. PEPIN at his death had been in pr
2 CONC ocess of building the empire, the Task difficulty: [ 18 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT     "In those times, to build the Kingdom from the aggregation of small states was itself north great difficulty ... But to keep the state intact after it had been FORMED was the colossal Task ... Each of the minor states had ... its little Soverei
2 CONC gn ... who ... gave himself chiefly to ... plotting, fighting and pillaging. "
2 CONT 
2 CONT Formation of a new Aquitania
2 CONT Main article: Aquitaine
2 CONT 
2 CONT Aquitania under Rome had been in Southern Gaul , Romanized and speaking the Romance language . Similar Hispania had been populated by peoples who spoke various languages, Including Celtic , but the area was now populated entirely by Romance languag
2 CONC e speakers. Between Aquitania and Hispania were the Euskaldunak, Latinized rate Vascones , or Basques , [ 19 ] living in the Basque Country, Vasconia, which extended, according to the distributions of place names attributable to the Basques, most d
2 CONC ensely in the Western Pyrenees but also cum lighthouses south cum the Upper Ebro River in Spain and Far North cum cum the Garonne River in France. [ 20 ] The French name, Gascony , derived from Vasconia. The Romans were never entirely subject to AB
2 CONC LE Vasconia. The parts they did, in which they placed the region's first cities, were sources of legions in the Roman army valued for their fighting abilities. The border with Aquitania was Toulouse .
2 CONT 
2 CONT At about 660 the Duchy of Vasconia united with the Duchy of Aquitania to form the Kingdom Under GaGa Felix of Aquitaine , Governing from Toulouse. This was the joint kingship with the 28-year-old Basque king, I. lupus . [ 21 ] The Kingdom was Sover
2 CONC eign and independent. On the one hand Vasconia fatten up predation to become a player on the field of European policy. On The Other, Whatever Felix had made ​​arrangements with the weak Merovingians were null and VOID. At Felix's death in 670 the j
2 CONC oint property of the kingship reverted entirely to lupus. As the Basques had north of joint inheritance law, but practiced primogeniture , lupus in effect founded the Basque hereditary dynasty of Kings of the Expanded Aquitania. [ 22 ]
2 CONT Acquisition of Aquitania by the Carolingians
2 CONT Further information: Umayyad Conquest of Hispania
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Latin Chronicles on the end of Visigothic Hispania Leave Much to Be like, Such denial of identifying characters, filling in the gaps, and reconciliation of numerous contradictions. [ 23 ] The Saracen (Muslim) sources, However, the present vie
2 CONC w more currants, Such denial in the Ta'rikh iftitah al-Andalus ("History of the Conquest of al-Andalus") by Ibn al-Qūṭiyya (a name meaning "the sound of the Gothic novel," referring to the granddaughter of the last king of all Visigothic Spain, wh
2 CONC o married the Saracen). Ibn al-Qūṭiyya, who had Another, Much longer name, Must have been relying to some degree on family oral tradition.
2 CONT 
2 CONT According to Ibn al-Qūṭiyya, [ 24 ] the last Visigothic king of the united Hispania died Before his three sons, Almund, Rómulo, and Ardabast, reached majority. Their engine was Regent at Toledo , but Roderic , Army chief of staff, staged the rebell
2 CONC ion, Capturing Cordova . Of all the possible outcomes, he's pavement to impose the joint rule over separate jurisdictions on the true heirs. Evidence of the division of some sort can be found in the distribution of coins imprinted with the name o
2 CONC f each king and the king in Lists. [ 25 ] Wittiza is succeeded by Roderic, who reigned for seven years and the half, followed by the certain Achila (Aquila), who reigned three and the half years. Provision reigns of BOTH terminated with the incursi
2 CONC on of the Saracens, then Roderic appears to have reigned the few years Before the majority of Achila. The latter's Kingdom is securely placed to the NORTHEAST, while Roderic seems to have taken the rest, noteworthy Portugal.
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Saracens crossed the mountains to claim ARDO's Septimania , only to encounter the Basque dynasty of Aquitania, always the allies all of the Goths. sacred the Great of Aquitania was at first Victorious at the Battle of Toulouse in 721. [ 26 ] Sa
2 CONC racen troops gradually massed in Septimania in 732 and advanced into Vasconia, and was sacred Defeated at the Battle of the River Garonne . They Took Bordeaux and were Advancing Towards Tours When sacred, powerless to stop them, appealed to his Arc
2 CONC h-Enemy, Charles Martel , Mayor of the Franks. In one of the first of the Lightning Marchese for which the Carolingian Kings became Farmhouse, Charles and his army appeared in the path of the Saracens between Tours and Poitiers , and in the Battl
2 CONC e of Tours settled the question of the Saracen advance into Europe. The Moors were Defeated jump conclusively that they retreated Across the mountains, never to return, leaving Septimania to Become part of Francia. [ 27 ] The sacred also had to str
2 CONC aw the price of incorporation into Charles's Kingdom, the decision that was repugnant to him and also to his heirs.
2 CONT Loss and recovery of Aquitania
2 CONT 
2 CONT After the death of his son, Hunald ALLIED himself with free LOMBARDY . However, sacred ambiguously had left the Kingdom jointly to his two sons, and Hunald Hatto. The latter, LOYAL to Francia, now went to war with his brother over full possession
2 CONC . Victorious, Hunald blinded and imprisoned his brother, only to be stricken by Fresh jump that he resigned and entered the Church as a monk back to Carolingian Penance according to sources. [ 28 ] His sound Waifer Took the early inheritance, becom
2 CONC ing Duke of Aquitania, and ratified the alliance with LOMBARDY. Waifer decided to honor it, repeating decimal his father's decision, which he justified by arguing that any Agreements with Charles Martel Martel became a pensioner's death. Since Aqui
2 CONC tania was now PEPIN's inheritance, [ clarification needed ] according to the latter peaked and his sound, the young Charles, hunted down Waifer, who could only Conduct the guerrilla war, and executed him. [ 29 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Among the contingents of the Frankish army were Bavarians under Tassilo III, Duke of Bavaria , in Agilofing , the hereditary Bavarian royal family. Grifo had installed himself cum Duke of Bavaria, buts PEPIN replaced him with a member of the roya
2 CONC l family yet the child, Tassilo, whose protector he had Become After the death of his father. The LOYALTY of the Agilolfings was perpetually in question, buts PEPIN exacted numerous oaths of LOYALTY from Tassilo. However, the latter had married Liu
2 CONC tperga , the daughter of Desiderius , King of LOMBARDY . At a critical point in the campaign Tassilo with all his Bavarians left the field. Out of reach of PEPIN, he repudiated all LOYALTY to Francia. [ 30 ] PEPIN had NO chance to respond and ill c
2 CONC um he Grew Within the few weeks after the fulfillment of Waifer died himself.
2 CONT 
2 CONT The first event of the brothers' reign was the Pricing of the Aquitainians and Gascons , in 769, in that Territory split between the two Kings. One Year Before, PEPIN had finally Defeated Waifer , Duke of Aquitaine , after waging the destructive, t
2 CONC hyme-year war against Aquitaine. Now, one Hunald (seemingly other than Hunald The Duke) led the Far North Aquitainians cum cum Angouleme . Charles fled Carloman, but Carloman refused to participate and returned to burgundy. Charles went to war, lea
2 CONC ding the army to Bordeaux , Where he set up the fort at Fronsac. Hunald was Forced to flee to the court of Duke lupus II of Gascony . Lupus, fearing Charles, TURNED Hunald over in exchange for peace, and he was put in the Monastery. Gascon Lords al
2 CONC so surrendered, and Aquitaine and Gascony were finally fully subdued by the Franks.
2 CONT Union perforce
2 CONT 
2 CONT The brothers Maintained lukewarm relations with the assistance of their engine Bertrada, buttes in 770 Charles the Treaty Signed with Duke Tassilo III of Bavaria and married the Lombard Princess (commonly Known today cum Desiderata ), the daughte
2 CONC r of King Desiderius , to surround Carloman with his own allies all. Though Pope Stephen III first opposed the marriage with the Lombard princess, he would soon have little to fear from the Frankish-Lombard alliance.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Less than the year after his marriage, Charlemagne repudiated Desiderata and quickly married the 13-year-old Swabian named Hildegard . The repudiated Desiderata returned to her father's court at pavia . Her father's wrath was now aroused, and he wo
2 CONC uld have gladly ALLIED with Carloman to Defeat Charles. Before any open hostilities could be said, However, Carloman died on 5 December 771, seemingly of Natural Causes. Carloman's widow Gerberga bloom to Desiderius' court in LOMBARDY with her ​​so
2 CONC ns for protection.
2 CONT Italian campaigns
2 CONT Conquest of the Lombard Kingdom
2 CONT The Frankish king Charlemagne was the devout Catholic and Maintained's close relationship with the papacy throughout his life. In 772, When Pope Adrian I was threatened by the Invaders, the king rushed to Rome to Provider assistance. Source, the do
2 CONC ll Letter Charlemagne for help at a meeting near Rome.
2 CONT 
2 CONT At his succession in 772, Pope Adrian I demanded the return of certain cities in the Former exarchate of Ravenna in accordance with the pledge at the succession of Desiderius. Instead, Desiderius Took over certain papal cities and invaded the Penta
2 CONC polis , HEADING for Rome. Adrian sent embassies to Charlemagne in autumn requesting he enforce the policies of his father, PEPIN. Desiderius sent his own embassies denying the pope's loads. The embassies BOTH put at Thionville , and Charlemagne uph
2 CONC eld the pope's side. Charlemagne demanded what the pope had requested, and Desiderius promptly swore never to comply. Charlemagne and his Uncle Bernard crossed the ALPS in 773 and chased the Lombards back to pavia, which they then besieged . [ 3
2 CONC 1 ] Charlemagne temporarily left to deal with the SIEGE Adelchis , son of Desiderius, who was Raising the army at Verona . The young prince was chased to the ADRIATIC coastlines, and he fled to Constantinople to plead for assistance from Constantin
2 CONC e V , who was waging war with Bulgaria . [ 32 ] [ 33 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT The SIEGE lasted until the spring of 774, When Charlemagne visited the pope in Rome. There he confirmed his father's Grants of land, [ 34 ] with peaked later Chronicles Thou speakest falsely claiming-that-he also Expanded them, granting Tuscany , E
2 CONC milia , Venice, and Corsica . The pope granted him the title patrician . He then returned to pavia, Where the Lombards were on the verge of surrendering. In return for their lives, the Lombards surrendered and opened the gates in early summer. Desi
2 CONC derius was sent to the Abbey of Corbie , and his son died in Constantinople Adelchis the patrician. Charles, unusually, had himself crowned with the Iron Crown and made ​​the magnates of LOMBARDY back homage to him at pavia. Only Duke Arechis II o
2 CONC f Benevento refused to submit and proclaimed independence. Charlemagne was then master of Italy cum king of the Lombards. He left Italy with the Garrison in pavia and the few Frankish Counts in place the Same year.
2 CONT 
2 CONT There was still instability, However, in Italy. In 776, Dukes of Friuli Hrodgaud and Hildeprand of Spoleto rebelled. Charlemagne rushed back from Saxony and Defeated The Duke of Friuli in battle; The Duke was slain. [ 33 ] The Duke of Spoleto Signe
2 CONC d the Treaty. Their co-conspirator, Arechis, was NOT subdued, and Adelchis, their candidate in Byzantium , never left that City. Northern Italy was now faithfully his.
2 CONT Southern Italy
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 787 Charlemagne directed his attention toward the Duchy of Benevento , Where Arechis was reigning Independently. Charlemagne besieged Salerno , and Arechis submitted to vassalage . However, with his death in 792, Benevento again proclaimed indep
2 CONC endence under his son Grimoald III . Grimoald was attacked by armies of Charles or his sons many times, but Charlemagne himself never returned to the Mezzogiorno , and never was Grimoald Forced to Surrender to Frankish suzerainty .
2 CONT Charles and his children
2 CONT Charlemagne (left) and Pippin the Hunchback . Tenth-belted copy of the lost original from about 830
2 CONT 
2 CONT During the first peace of any substantial length (780-782), Charles began to appoint his sons to positions of authority Within the realm, in the tradition of the Kings and mayors of the past. In 781, he made ​​his two younger sons Kings, having the
2 CONC m crowned by the Pope. The elder of these two, Carloman , was made ​​king of Italy , Tracking the Iron Crown which his father had first worn in 774, and in the same Cremona was renamed "Pippin." [ 33 ] [ 34 ] The younger of the two, Louis , becam
2 CONC e king of Aquitaine . Charlemagne ordered Pippin and Louis to be raised in the customs of their Kingdoms, and he gave their control of their subkingdoms Regents peaked, but real power was always in his hands, though he intended his sons to inheri
2 CONC t their realms some day. Nor did he tolerate insubordination in his sons: in 792, he banished his eldest, though illegitimate possible, sound, Pippin the Hunchback , to the monastery of Prüm, because the young man had joined the rebellion against h
2 CONC im.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charles was determined to have his children educated, Including his Daughters, cum he himself was scored. His children were taught all the arts, and his Daughters were learned in the Way of Being the novel. Took his sons archery, horsemanship, An
2 CONC d Other Outdoor activities.
2 CONT Instructing Charlemagne Louis the Pious
2 CONT 
2 CONT The sons fought many wars on behalf of their father When they came of age. Charles was preoccupied with the Bretton Mostly, whose border he Shared insurrected on at least two occasions and who were easily and putt down, but he was also sent agains
2 CONC t the Saxons a byte occasions. In 805 and 806, he was sent into the Böhmerwald (modern Bohemia ) to deal with the Slavs living there (Bohemian Tribes, ancestor of the modern Czechs ). He subjected them to Frankish authority and devastated the valle
2 CONC y of the Elbe, forcing a tribute to them. Pippin had to hold the Avar and Beneventan Borders buts also fought the Slavs to his north. He was uniquely poised to Re the Byzantium Empire When that finally Conflict arose after Charlemagne's imperial co
2 CONC ronation and the Venetian rebellion. Finally, Louis was in charge of the Spanish March and also went to Southern Italy to Re The Duke of Benevento on at least one occasions. He Took Barcelona in a great SIEGE in 797 (see below).
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne's attitude toward his Daughters has been the subject of discussion Much. He kept them at home with him and refused to Allow them to contract sacramental marriages - presale possible rate the creation of cadet branches of the family to c
2 CONC hallenge the main line, cum had been the case with Tassilo of Bavaria - yet he tolerated their extramarital relationships , even rewarding their common-law Husbands, and treasured the illegitimate Grandchildren they produced for him. He also, appar
2 CONC ently, refused to Believe stories of their wild behavior. After his death the surviving Daughters were banished from the court by their brother, Louis the Pious, to take up residence in the convents they had been bequeathed by their father. At leas
2 CONC t one of them, Bertha, had to recognised relationship, if NOT the marriage, with Angilbert , a member of Charlemagne's court circle. [ 35 ] [ 36 ]
2 CONT Carolingian expansion to the south
2 CONT See also: Abbasid-Carolingian alliance
2 CONT Vasconia and the Pyrenees
2 CONT 
2 CONT The destructive war led by PEPIN in Aquitaine, brought Although rate Satisfactory conclusion for the Franks, Proved the Frankish power structure south of the Loire was feeble and unreliable. After the Defeat and death of Waifer of Aquitaine in 768
2 CONC , while Aquitaine submitted again to the Carolingian dynasty, a new rebellion Broke out in 769 led by Hunald II, maybe the sound of Waifer. He Took Refuge with the Ally Duke lupus II of Gascony , but probably out of fear of Charlemagne's reprisal
2 CONC , handed him over to the new King of the Franks Besides pledging LOYALTY to him, which seemed to confirm the peace in the Basque area south of the Garonne.
2 CONT 
2 CONT However, wary of new Basque uprisings, Charlemagne seems to have tried to diminish Duke lupus's power by appointing the certain SEGUIN cum Count of Bordeaux (778) And Other Counts of Frankish background in bordering Areas ( Toulouse , County of Féz
2 CONC ensac ), the decision that seriously undermined the authority of the Duke of Gascony (Vasconia). The Basque Duke in turn seems to have contributed decisively or schemed the Battle of Roncevaux Pass (referred to cum "Basque treachery"). The Defeat o
2 CONC f Charlemagne's army in Roncevaux (778) confirmed him in his determination to rule directly by Establishing the Kingdom of Aquitaine (sounds Louis the Pious proclaimed first king) based on the power base of Frankish officials, Distributing Lands am
2 CONC ong colonisers and allocating Lands rate the Church, which he Took cum Ally. A program was put on Christianization place Across the high Pyrenees (778).
2 CONT 
2 CONT The New Political Arrangement for Vasconia didn't site well with the local Lords. As we hear of 788 of Adalric fighting and Capturing Chorson , Carolingian Count of Toulouse. He was eventually Released, but Charlemagne, enraged at the compromise, d
2 CONC ecided to infuse him and appointed his trustee William of Orange . William in turn fought the Basques and Defeated them after banishing Adalric (790).
2 CONT 
2 CONT From 781 ( Pallars , Ribagorça ) to 806 ( Pamplona under Frankish influential), Tracking the County of Toulouse for a power base, Charlemagne managed to assert Frankish authority over the Pyrenees by bringing to heel the south-western Marchese of T
2 CONC oulouse (790) and Establishing vassal counties on the Southern Pyrenees that were marked up to the Marca Hispanica . As of 794, we hear for the first time of the Frankish vassal, the Basque lords Belasko ( al-Galashki , 'the Gaul') in the Lands o
2 CONC f Álava , but remained in Pamplona Cordovan and local hands up to 806. Belasko and the counties in the Marca Hispánica provided the necessary springboard to attack the Andalusians (Expedition led by William Count of Toulouse and Louis the Pious t
2 CONC o capture Barcelona in 801), in the way that Charlemagne had succeeded in expanding the Carolingian rule all around the Pyrenees by 812 , Although events in the Duchy of Vasconia (rebellion in Pamplona, ​​Count overthrown in Aragon , Duke of Bordea
2 CONC ux deposed SEGUIN, Pricing of the Basque Lords, etc..) were to prove it ephemeral on his death.
2 CONT Roncesvalles campaign
2 CONT 
2 CONT According to the Muslim historian Ibn al-Athir , the Diet of Paderborn had received the representatives of the Muslim rulers of Zaragoza , Girona , Barcelona, ​​and Huesca . Their masters had been cornered in the Iberian Peninsula by Abd ar-Rahma
2 CONC n I , the Umayyad Emir of Cordova . These "Saracen" ( Moorish and Muladi ) rulers offered their homage to the great king of the Franks in return for military support. Seeing the opportunity to extinguish Christendom and his own power and believin
2 CONC g the Saxons to be the fully conquered nation, Charlemagne agreed to go to Spain.
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 778, he led the army Neustrian Across the Western Pyrenees , while the Austrasians, Lombards, and Burgundians passed over the Eastern Pyrenees. The armies fled at Saragossa and Charlemagne received the homage of the Muslim rulers, Sulayman al-Sa
2 CONC udi and Kasmin ibn Yusuf, but the City did NOT fall for him. Indeed, Charlemagne was facing the toughest battle of his career Where the Muslims had the upper hand and Forced him to Retreat. He decided to go home, since he could NOT trust the Basque
2 CONC s , whom he had subdued by conquering Pamplona . He TURNED to leave Iberia, but he was cum Passing through the Pass of Roncesvalles one of the most Farmhouse events of his long reign occurred. The Basques fell on his rearguard and baggage train, ut
2 CONC terly destroying it. The Battle of Roncevaux Pass , though less than the battle to Mere skirmish, left many Farmhouse dead: among which were the seneschal Eggihard, the Count of the palace Anselm, and the warden of the Breton March , Roland , inspi
2 CONC ring the subsequent creation of the Song of Roland ( La Chanson de Roland ).
2 CONT Wars with the Moors
2 CONT Harun al-Rashid Receiving the delegation of Charlemagne in Baghdad , by Julius Köckert (1864)
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Conquest of Italy brought Charlemagne in contact with the Saracens who, at the time, controlled the Mediterranean . Pippin, his son, was Much occupied with Saracens in Italy. Charlemagne conquered Corsica and Sardinia at the dating and gender i
2 CONC n 799 the Balearic Islands . The islands were attacked by Saracen pirates OFTEN, but the Counts of Genoa and Tuscany ( Boniface ) kept them at given with large fleets until the end of Charlemagne's reign. Charlemagne even had contact with the calip
2 CONC hal court in Baghdad . In 797 (or 801 possible), the caliph of Baghdad, Harun al-Rashid , Charlemagne Printed with an Asian Elephant named Abul-Abbas and the clock . [ 37 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT In Hispania , the Struggle against the Moors continued unabated throughout the latter half of his reign. His son Louis was in charge of the Spanish border. In 785, his hands and permanently capture Girona Extended Frankish control into the Catala
2 CONC n littoral for the duration of Charlemagne's reign (and Much longer, it remained nominally Frankish until the Treaty of Corbeil in 1258). The Chief-Muslims in the NORTHEAST of Islamic Spain were constantly revolting against Cordovan Authority, an
2 CONC d they OFTEN TURNED to the Franks for help. The Frankish border was slowly extended until 795, When Girona, Cardona , Ausona , and Urgell were united into the new Spanish March , Within the old duchy of Septimania .
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 797 Barcelona , the greatest City of the region, fell to the Franks When Zeid, its governor, rebelled against Cordova and, Failing, handed it to them. The Umayyad authority recaptured it in 799. However, Louis of Aquitaine market the entire Arm
2 CONC y of his Kingdom over the Pyrenees and besieged it for two years, wintering there from 800 to 801, When it capitulated. The Franks continued to press forward against the Emir . They Took Tarragona in 809 and Tortosa in 811. The Last Conquest brough
2 CONC t them to the mouth of the Ebro and gave them raiding access to Valencia , prompting the Emir al-Hakam I to recognize their conquests in 812.
2 CONT Eastern campaigns
2 CONT Saxon Wars
2 CONT Map Showing Charlemagne's Additions (in light green) to the Frankish Kingdom
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne was engaged in almost constant battle throughout his reign, [ 38 ] OFTEN at the head of his elite scara bodyguard squadrons, with his sword Legendary joyful in hand. In the Saxon Wars , spanning thirty years and eighteen battles, he con
2 CONC quered Saxonia and proceeded to convert the conquered to Christianity.
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Germanic Saxons were Divider into four subgroups in four regions. Nearest to Austrasia was Westphalia and furthest away was Eastphalia . In between these two Kingdoms was that of Engria and north of these three, at the base of the Jutland Penin
2 CONC sula, was Nordalbingia .
2 CONT 
2 CONT In his first campaign, Charlemagne Forced Engrians in the 773 to submit and cut down the Irminsul pillar near Paderborn . [ 39 ] The campaign was cut short by his first Expedition to Italy. He returned in 775, Marching through Westphalia and conque
2 CONC ring the Saxon fort of Sigiburg . He then crossed Engria, Where he Defeated the Saxons again. Finally, in Eastphalia, he Defeated the Saxon force, and its leader Hessi converted to Christianity. Charlemagne returned through Westphalia, leaving enca
2 CONC mpments at Sigiburg and Eresburg , which had been important Saxon bastions. All of Saxony but Nordalbingia was under his control, but Saxon resistance had NOT ended.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Following his campaign in Italy to subjugate the Dukes of Friuli and Spoleto, Charlemagne returned very rapidly to Saxony in 776, Where the rebels had destroyed his Fortress at Eresburg. The Saxons were once again brought to heel, but their main le
2 CONC ader, Widukind , managed to escape to Denmark, home of his wife. Charlemagne built a new camp at Karlstadt . In 777, he called the national diet at Paderborn to integrate Saxony fully into the Frankish Kingdom. Many Saxons were baptised cum Christi
2 CONC an.
2 CONT 
2 CONT In the summer of 779, he again invaded Saxony and reconquered Eastphalia, Engria, and Westphalia. At a diet near Lippe , he Divider Missionary District and into the land himself Assisted in several mass baptisms (780). He then returned to Italy and
2 CONC , for the first time, there was NO Immediate Saxon revolt. Peaceful Saxony was from 780 to 782.
2 CONT Charlemagne (742-814) Receiving the submission of Widukind at Paderborn in 785, by Ary Scheffer (1795-1858). Versailles
2 CONT 
2 CONT He returned to Saxony in 782 and instituted the code of law and appointed Counts, BOTH Saxon and Frank. The laws were draconian on religious issues; For example, the two Capitulatio partibus Saxoniae prescrit death rate Saxon pagans who refused t
2 CONC o convert to Christianity. This revived the renewal of the Olde Conflict. That year, in autumn, Widukind returned and led a new revolt. In response, at werden in Lower Saxony, Charlemagne is Recorded cum having ordered the outturn of 4,500 Saxon pr
2 CONC isoners, Known cum the massacre of werden ("Verdener Blutgericht"). The killings triggered three years of renewed bloody Warfare (783-785). During this war the Frisians were also finally subdued and the large part of their Fleet was burned. The wa
2 CONC r ended with Widukind accepting baptism.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Thereafter, the Saxons Maintained the peace for seven years, but in 792 the Westphalians again rose against their conquerors. The Eastphalians Nordalbingians and joined them in 793, but the insurrection did NOT catch on and was put down by 794. Eng
2 CONC rian In rebellion followed in 796, but the presence of Charlemagne, Christian Saxons and Slavs quickly CRUSHED it. The last insurrection of the independent-minded people occurred in 804, more than thirty years after Charlemagne's first campaign aga
2 CONC inst them. This time, the most restive of them, the Nordalbingians, found themselves effectively disempowered from rebellion for the time Being. According to Einhard:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     The war that had lasted many years was at length ended by jump their acceding to the terms offered by the King; which were renunciation of their national religious customs and the worship of Devils, Acceptance of the sacraments of the Christia
2 CONC n faith and religion, and union with the Franks to form one people.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Submission of Bavaria
2 CONT 
2 CONT By 774 AD Charlemagne invaded the Kingdom of LOMBARDY , he later annexed the Lombardian Territories and assumed its crown, placing the papal States under Frankish protection. [ 40 ] The Duchy of Spoleto was also acquired south of Rome in 774, whil
2 CONC e in the central Western parts of Europe, the Duchy of Bavaria was absorbed and the Bavarian continued policy of Establishing tributary Marchese , (Borders protected in return for tribute or taxes) among the Slavic Serbs, and Czechs. The remainin
2 CONC g power confronting the Franks in the East were the Avars , Slavs However Charlemagne went on Acquiring Other Areas, Including Bohemia , Macedonia , Moravia , Austria and Croatia . [ 40 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 789, Charlemagne TURNED his attention to Bavaria . He claimed Tassilo was the unfit ruler, due to his oath-breaking. The loads were exaggerated, but Tassilo was deposed anyway and put in the Monastery of Jumièges . In 794, he was made ​​to renou
2 CONC nce any claim to Bavaria for himself and his family (the Agilolfings ) at the synod of Frankfurt . Bavaria was subdivided into Frankish counties, cum had been with decimal Saxony.
2 CONT Avar campaigns
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 788, the Avars , the PAGAN Asian horde that had settled down in what is today Hungary (Einhard called them Huns ), invaded Friuli and Bavaria. Charlemagne was preoccupied with other matters until 790, When He Emporium down the Danube and laid wa
2 CONC ste to the Avar Territory Gyor . A Lombard army under Pippin then into the market Drava valley and ravaged Pannonia . The campaigns would have continued if the Saxons had NOT revolted again in 792, breaking seven years of peace.
2 CONT 
2 CONT For the next two years, Charlemagne was occupied, along with the Slavs, against the Saxons. Pippin and Duke Eric of Friuli continued, However, the rate Assault Avars' Ring-shaped strongholds. The great Ring of the Avars, their capital Fortress, wa
2 CONC s taken twice. The Booty was sent to Charlemagne at his capital, Aachen , and redistributed to all his followers and even to foreign rulers, Including King Offa of Mercia . Soon the Avar tuduns had lost the Will to Re and Traveller to Aachen to sub
2 CONC ject themselves to Charlemagne cum and Christian vassals. Charlemagne accepted their Surrender Saintes and one native chief, baptised Abraham, back to Avaria with the former title of khagan . Abraham kept his people in line, but in 800, the Bulgari
2 CONC ans under Khan KRUM also attacked the remains of Avar state.
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 803 Charlemagne's Holy HUGE Bavarian army into Pannonia , defeating and bringing an end to the Avar confederation . [ 41 ] In November of the Same year, Charlemagne went to Regensburg Where the Avar leaders acknowledged him cum their own ruler
2 CONC . [ 41 ] In 805 the Avar khagan, who had already been baptised, went to Aachen to ask permission to settle with his people south-eastward from Vienna . [ 41 ] The Transdanubian Territories became text parts of the Frankish realm, which was abolishe
2 CONC d by the Magyars in 899 -900.
2 CONT Northeast Slavs Expeditions
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 789, in recognition of his new PAGAN neighbours, the Slavs , Charlemagne's Emporium Austrasian-Saxon army Across the Elbe into Obotrite Territory. The Slavs immediately submitted, led by their leader Witzin. Charlemagne then accepted the Surrend
2 CONC er of the Wiltzes Under Dragovit and demanded many hostages. Charlemagne also demanded the permission to send missionaries into this region PAGAN unmolested. The Army contract to the Baltic Before Turning around and Marching to the Rhine, winning M
2 CONC uch Booty with NO harassment. The tributary Slavs became LOYAL allies all. In 795, the Saxons When Broke The Peace, and the Abotrites Wiltzes lather in Arms with their new master against the Saxons. Witzin died in battle and Charlemagne avenged hi
2 CONC m by harrying the Eastphalians on the Elbe. Thrasuco, his successor, led his hands to Conquest over the Nordalbingians and handed their leaders over to Charlemagne, who greatly honoured him. The LOYAL Abotrites remained until Charles' death and fou
2 CONC ght later against the Danes.
2 CONT Southeast Slavs Expeditions
2 CONT Europe around 800
2 CONT 
2 CONT When Charlemagne Incorporated Much of Central Europe, he brought the Frankish state face to face with the Avars and Slavs in the SOUTHEAST. [ 42 ] The most SOUTHEAST Frankish Neighbors were Croats , who settled in Pannonian Croatia and Dalmatian Cr
2 CONC oatia . While fighting the Avars, the Franks had called for their support. [ 43 ] During the 790s, When Charlemagne campaigned against the Avars, he tower major victory in 796. [ 44 ] Pannonian Croatian Duke of Pannonian Croatia Vojnomir Aided Char
2 CONC lemagne, and the Franks made ​​themselves overlords over the Croatians of Northern Dalmatia, Slavonia , and Pannonia. [ 44 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Frankish Commander Eric of Friuli wanted to quench his dominion by conquering coastlines Croatian Duchy. During that time, Croatia was ruled by Duke Dalmatian Višeslav of Croatia , who was one of the first Croatian Dukes Known. [ 45 ] In the Ba
2 CONC ttle of Trsat , the forces of Eric fled their positions and were totally routed by the forces of Višeslav. [ 45 ] Eric himself was among the killed, and his death and Defeat Proved a great Blow for the Carolingian Empire. [ 42 ] [ 45 ] [ 46 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne also directed his attention to the Slavs to the west of the Avar khaganate: the Carantanians and Carniolans . These people were subdued by the Lombards and Bavarii, were made ​​tributaries, but were never fully Incorporated into the Fra
2 CONC nkish state.
2 CONT Imperium
2 CONT Coronation
2 CONT Charlemagne's chapel at Aachen Cathedral
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 799, Pope Leo III had been mistreated by the Romans, who tried to put out his eyes and tear out his tongue. Leo getaway and bloom to Charlemagne at Paderborn , Raskin him to intervene in Rome and restore him. Charlemagne, advised by scholar Alcu
2 CONC in of York , agreed to travel to Rome, doing jumps in November 800 and holding the Council on 1 December. On 23 December Leo swore the oath of innocence. At Mass , on Christmas Day (25 December), When Charlemagne knelt at the Altar to pray, the Pop
2 CONC e crowned him Imperator Romanorum ("Emperor of the Romans") in Saint Peter's Basilica . In doing jumps, the Pope was effectively nullifying the legitimacy of Empress Irene of Constantinople :
2 CONT 
2 CONT     "When Odoacer compelled the abdication of Romulus Augustulus , he did NOT abolish the Western Empire as a separate power, but cause it to be reunited with five or into the Eastern, jump from that time that there was GaGa's undivided Roman Empir
2 CONC e ... [Pope Leo III and Charlemagne], like their predecessors, held the Roman Empire to be one and indivisible, and Proposed by the coronation of [Charlemagne] NOT to proclaim the SEVERANCE of the East and West ... they were NOT revolting against t
2 CONC he reigning Sovereign, but legitimately filling up the place of the deposed Constantine VI  ... [Charlemagne] was held to be the legitimate successor, scores of Romulus Augustulus, but of Constantine VI ... " [ 47 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne's coronation cum Emperor, though intended to represent the CONTINUATION of the unbroken line of Emperors from Augustus to Constantine VI, had the effect of setting up two separate (and opposing OFTEN) Empires and too separate claims t
2 CONC o imperial authority. For centuries to come, the Emperors of BOTH West and East would mark competing claims of sovereignty over the whole.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Einhard says that Charlemagne was ignorant of the Pope's Intent Forms are available and did NOT want any Such coronation:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     [H] e at first had Such aversion is that he said that he would NOT have set foot in the Church the day that they [the imperial Titles] were conferred, Although it was a great feast-day, if he could have foreseen the Design of the Pope.
2 CONT 
2 CONT A number of modern Scholar, However, [ 48 ] suggests that Charlemagne was indeed Aware of the coronation; certainly he cannot have missed the bejeweled crown waiting on the Altar When He came to pray; Something even contemporary sources support ana
2 CONC lysed When thoroughly. [ 49 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT In any event, Charlemagne used these circumstances to claim that he was the renewer of the Roman Empire, which had apparently fallen into degradation under the Byzantines . In his official Carter, Charles preferred the style Karolus Magnus sereniss
2 CONC imus Augustus the DEO coronatus pacificus imperator Romanum gubernans imperium [ 50 ] ("Charles, Most Serene Augustus crowned by God, the great, Peaceful Emperor ruling the Roman empire") to the more direct Imperator Romanorum ("Emperor of the Roma
2 CONC ns").
2 CONT Imperial diplomatic
2 CONT Europe around 814
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Iconoclasm layout of the Byzantine Isaurian dynasty was endorsed by the Franks. [ 51 ] The Second Council of Nicaea reintroduced the veneration of icons under Empress Irene . The council was NOT recognized by Charlemagne since north Frankish em
2 CONC issaries had been invited, even though Charlemagne ruled more than three provinces of the old Roman empire and was considered equal to the Byzantine Emperor in Rank. And while the Pope Supported the reintroduction of the iconic veneration, he polit
2 CONC ically digressed from Byzantium. [ 51 ] He certainly want to Increase the influential of the papacy, to his Saviour honour Charlemagne, and to Solve the constitutional issues then most troubling to European jurists in the era When Rome was NOT in t
2 CONC he hands of the Emperor. Thus, Charlemagne's assumption of the imperial title was NOT the usurpation in the eyes of the Franks or Italians. It was, However, Seen Such denial in Byzantium, Where it was protested by Irene and her successor Nicephoru
2 CONC s I - neither of whom had any great effect in enforcing their protests.
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Byzantines, However, still held several Territories in Italy: Venice (what was left of the Exarchate of Ravenna ), Reggio (in Calabria ), Brindisi (in Apulia ), and Naples (the Ducatus Neapolitanus ). These regions remained outside of Frankis
2 CONC h hands until 804, When the Venetians, torn by infighting, Transferred their allegiance to the Iron Crown of Pippin, Charles' sound. The Pax Nicephori ended. Nicephorus ravaged the coasts with the Fleet, initiating the only instance of-war betwee
2 CONC n the Byzantines and the Franks. The Conflict lasted until 810, When the pro-Byzantine party in Venice gave their City back to the Byzantine Emperor, and the two emperors of Europe made ​​peace: Charlemagne received the Istrian Peninsula and in 81
2 CONC 2 the Emperor Michael I Rhangabes recognised his cum Stratus Emperor , [ 52 ] Although NOT necessarily cum "Emperor of the Romans". [ 53 ]
2 CONT Danish attacks
2 CONT 
2 CONT After the Conquest of Nordalbingia, the Frankish frontier was brought into contact with Scandinavia. The PAGAN Danes, "the race almost unknown to his ancestor, but destined to be only too well Known to his sons" as Charles Oman described them, inha
2 CONC biting the Jutland Peninsula, had heard many stories from Widukind and his allies all who had taken Refuge with them about the dangers of the Franks and the Fury which their Christian king could direct against PAGAN neighbours.
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 808, the king of the Danes, Godfred , built the vast Danevirke Across the isthmus of Schleswig . This Defence, last employed in the Danish-Prussian War of 1864, was at its beginning at 30 km (19 mi) long earthenwork RAMPART. The Danish Danevirk
2 CONC e protected moors and gave Godfred the opportunity to harass Frisia and Flanders with pirate raids. He also subdued the Frank-ALLIED Wiltzes and fought the Abotrites.
2 CONT Portion of the 814 death shroud of Charlemagne. It represents the quadriga and was manufactured in Constantinople .
2 CONT 
2 CONT Godfred invaded Frisia, joked of visiting Aachen, but was murdered Before he could back any more repeats by the Frankish assassinates or by one of his own hands. Godfred was succeeded by his NEPHEW Hemming , who concluded the Treaty of Heiligen ear
2 CONC th with Charlemagne in 811.
2 CONT Death
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 813, Charlemagne called Louis the Pious , king of Aquitaine , his only surviving legitimate son, to his court. There Charlemagne crowned his son with his own hands cum co-Emperor and sent him back to Aquitaine. He then spent the autumn hunting B
2 CONC efore Returning to Aachen on 1 November. In January, he fell ill with pleurisy . [ 54 ] In deep depression (Mostly because many of his plans were NOT YET realize), he Took to his bed on 21 January and cum Einhard tells it:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     He died January twenty-EIGHTH, the seventh day from the time that he Took to his bed, at Nine o'clock in the morning, after partaking of the Holy Communion , in the Twenty-second year of his age and the forty-seventh of his reign.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Frederick II's gold and silver casket for Charlemagne, the Karlsschrein
2 CONT Persephone sarcophagus of Charlemagne
2 CONT 
2 CONT He was buried the Same Day cum his death, in Aachen Cathedral , Although the cold weather and the Nature of Illness made ​​his hurried Burial Such is unnecessary. The earliest surviving planctus , the two Planctus obitu Karoli , was composed by th
2 CONC e monk of Bobbio , which he had patronised. [ 55 ] A later story, told by Otho of Lomello, Count of the Palace at Aachen in the time of Otto III , would claim that he and Emperor Otto had discovered Charlemagne's Tomb: the Emperor, they claimed, wa
2 CONC s seated Upon the throne, wearing the crown and holding the sceptre, his flesh almost entirely incorrupt. In 1165, Frederick I re-opened the Tomb again and placed in the Emperor's sarcophagus beneath the floor of the Cathedral. [ 56 ] In 1215 Frede
2 CONC rick II re-interred him in the casket made ​​of gold and silver.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne's death greatly Affected many of his Subjects, particularly those of the Literary Clique who had surrounded him at Aachen . The anonymous monk of Bobbio lamented: [ 57 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT     From the Lands Where the Sun Rises to Western shores, People are crying and wailing ... the Franks, the Romans, all Christian, are stung with Mourning and great worry ... the young and old, Glorious nobility, all lament the loss of their Caesa
2 CONC r ... the world laments the death of Charles ... O Christ, you who Govern the Heavenly host, Grant's Peaceful place to Charles in your Kingdom. ALAS for paltry May.
2 CONT 
2 CONT He was succeeded by his surviving son, Louis, who had been crowned the previous year. His empire lasted only Another generation in its entirety; its division, according to custom, between Louis's own sons after their father's death laid the Foundat
2 CONC ion for the modern states of Germany and France. [ 58 ]
2 CONT Administration
2 CONT 
2 CONT As the administrator, Charlemagne stalls out for his many reforms: Monetary , governmental, military, cultural, and ecclesiastical . He is the main protagonist of the "Carolingian Renaissance."
2 CONT Military
2 CONT 
2 CONT It has long been held that the dominance of Charlemagne's military was based on a " cavalry revolution "led by Charles Martel in 730s. However, the Stirrup , which made ​​the "shock cavalry" lance charge possible, was NOT introduced to the Frankis
2 CONC h Kingdom until the earth EIGHTH belt. [ 59 ] Instead, Charlemagne's success rested primarily on Novel SIEGE technologies and excellent logistics. [ 60 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT However, large numbers of horses were used by the Frankish military During the age of Charlemagne. This was because horses provided the quick, long-distance method of transporting troops , which was critical to building and maintaining the Such exp
2 CONC ansive empire. [ 59 ]
2 CONT Economic and Monetary Reform
2 CONT Monogram of Charlemagne, from the Subscription of the royal diploma: Signum (monogr.: KAROLVS) Karoli regis gloriosissimi
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne had the significant role in determining the economic future of Europe Immediate. Pursuing his father's reforms, Charlemagne abolished the Monetary system based on the gold on , and he and the Anglo-Saxon King Offa of Mercia Took up th
2 CONC e system set in place by Pippin. There were strong pragmatic reasons for this abandonment of the gold standard , the notable shortage of gold itself.
2 CONT 
2 CONT The gold shortage was the direct consequence of the conclusion of peace with Byzantium, which result in ceding of Venice and Sicily to the East and losing their trade routes to Africa. The resulting standardisation economically harmonizing and unif
2 CONC ied the complex Array of Currencies which had been in use at the commencement of his reign, thus simplifying trade and commerce.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne established a new standard, the indulging carolinienne (from the Latin Libra , the modern pound ), which was based Upon the pound of silver-a unit of BOTH money and weight-which was worth 20 sources (from the Latin solidus [which was pr
2 CONC imarily in accounting unscrew and never actually minted], the modern shilling ) or 240 deniers (from the Latin denarius , the modern Penny ). During this period, the delivery and the on were rhymes units; Only the last was the coin of the realm.
2 CONT Latest from the era of Charlemagne, Tours , 793-812
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne instituted principles for accounting practice by means of the two Capitulare villis of 802, which laid down strict rules for the way in which incomes and expenses were to be Recorded.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Early in Charlemagne's rule he tacitly allowed the Jews to monopolize money lending. Then lending money for interest was proscribed in 814, Being against Church law at the time, Charlemagne introduced the Capitulary for the Jews , the prohibition o
2 CONC n Jews Eingang in money-lending due to the religious convictions of the majority of his constituents, in fueling BANNING Across the board it. A reversal of his earlier Recorded general policy. [ 61 ] In ADDITION to this macro-oriented reform of th
2 CONC e economy, Charlemagne also Performer's significant number of microeconomic reform, cum Such direct control of prices and levies on certain goods and COMMODITIES.
2 CONT 
2 CONT His Capitulary for the Jews , However was NOT representative of his Overall economic relationship or attitude, toward the Frankish Jews and certainly NOT his earlier relationship with them, which had evolved over his lifespan. His paid personal phy
2 CONC sician for example was Jewish, [ 62 ] he employed at least one Jew for his diplomatic mission, [ 63 ] Isaac was his personal representative to the Muslim caliphate of Baghdad. Letters have been credited to him cum well inviting Jews to settle in hi
2 CONC s Kingdom, for economic purpose, generally welcoming them through his Overall, incremental policies. [ 64 ] [ 65 ] [ 66 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne applied this system to Much of the European continent, and Offa's standard was voluntarily adopted by Much of England. After Charlemagne's death, continental coinage degraded, and most of Europe resorted to using the continued high-qual
2 CONC ity English coin until about 1100.
2 CONT Education Reform
2 CONT 
2 CONT A part of Charlemagne's success cum ruler and warrior and administrator can be traced to his admiration for learning and education. His reign and the era it ushered in are OFTEN referred to cum the Carolingian Renaissance because of the flowering o
2 CONC f Scholarship, literature, art, and architecture which characterized it. Charlemagne, brought into contact with the culture and learning of other Country (especially Visigothic Spain, Anglo-Saxon England, and Lombard Italy) due to his vast conquest
2 CONC s, greatly increased the provision of monastic schools and scriptoria (centers for book-copying) in Francia.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Most of the presently surviving works of classical Latin were copied and reserved by Carolingian Scholar. Indeed, the earliest Manuscript texts are available for many former Carolingian. It is almost certain that the text which survived to the Caro
2 CONC lingian age still survive.
2 CONT 
2 CONT The pan-European Nature of Charlemagne's influential is indicated by the origin of many of the hands who worked for him: Alcuin , the Anglo-Saxon from York ; Theodulf , the Visigoth , probably from Septimania ; Paul the Deacon , Lombard; Peter of P
2 CONC ISA and Paulinus of Aquileia , Italians ; and Angilbert , Angilram , Einhard , and Waldo of Reichenau , Franks.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Took Charlemagne's serious interest in Scholarship, Promoting the liberal arts at the court, that his Children and Grandchildren Ordering eBay well-educated, and even studying himself (in the time When even the leaders who promote education did NO
2 CONC T take time to learn themselves) under the tutelage of Peter of PISA, from whom he learned Grammar; Alcuin, with whom he studied rhetoric, dialectic (logic), and astronomers (he was particularly interested in the movements of the stars); and Einhar
2 CONC d, who Assisted him in his studies of arithmetic. [ 67 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT His great scholarly Failure, cum Einhard relates, was his disqualification to write: When in his old age he began attempts to learn-practicing the formation of letters in his bed During his free time on books and wax tablets he hid under his PILLOW
2 CONC -" his effort came too earth in life and achieved little success ", and his Ability to read - which Einhard is silent about, and which tolerates NO contemporary source-has also been called into question. [ 67 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 800, Charlemagne enlarged the Hostel at the Muristan in Jerusalem and added it to the library. He certainly had NOT been personally in Jerusalem. [ 68 ] [ 69 ]
2 CONT Church reform
2 CONT 
2 CONT Unlike his father, Pippin, and Uncle, Carloman, Charlemagne Expanded the reform programs of the Church. The deepening of the spiritual life was later to Be Seen cum central to public policy and royal Governance. His reforms focused on the strengthe
2 CONC ning of the Church's power structure, improving the skill and moral quality of the Clergy, standardizing liturgical practices, improvements on the basic tenets of the faith and morals, and the rooting out of paganism. His authority was now extende
2 CONC d over Church and State. He could discipline clerics, control ecclesiastical property and defines Orthodox doctrine. Despite the harsh Legislation and sudden change, he had support from the Radio's Well Developed Clergy who approved his wish to dee
2 CONC pen the piety and morals of his Christian Subjects. [ 2 ]
2 CONT See also: Charlemagne and church music
2 CONT Writing Reform
2 CONT Page from the Lorsch Gospel of Charlemagne's reign
2 CONT 
2 CONT During Charles' reign, the Roman half uncial script and its cursive version, which had given rise Gently rate various continental minuscule scripts, were combined with features from the script INSULAR Being that were used in Irish and English monas
2 CONC teries. Carolingian minuscule was created Rain Under the employers of Charlemagne. Alcuin of York, who rank the palace school and scriptorium at Aachen, was probably the chief influential in this.
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Revolutionary character of the Carolingian reform, However, can be over-emphasised; endeavors at taming the crabbed Merovingian and Germanic hands had been underway Before Alcuin arrived at Aachen. The new minuscule was disseminated first fro
2 CONC m Aachen and later from the influential scriptorium at Tours , Where the Abbot Alcuin Retired cum.
2 CONT Political Reform
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne engaged in many reforms of Frankish Governance, but he continued also in many traditional practices, the denial Such division of the Kingdom among sons. [ citation needed ]
2 CONT Organization
2 CONT Main article: Government of the Carolingian Empire
2 CONT 
2 CONT The Carolingian king exercised the bannum , the right to rule and command. He had supreme jurisdiction in judicial matters, made ​​Legislation, led the army, and protected BOTH the Church and the Poor. His administration was the attempt to organiz
2 CONC e the Kingdom, church, and nobility around him. However, the effort was heavily dependent Upon the efficiency, LOYALTY, and support of his Subjects. [ citation needed ]
2 CONT Imperial coronation
2 CONT Imperial Coronation of Charlemagne, by Friedrich Kaulbach , 1861
2 CONT 
2 CONT Historians have struggled for centuries whether Charlemagne was Aware of the Pope's Intent Forms are available to crown him Emperor prior to the coronation (Charlemagne declared that he would NOT have entered Saint Peter's had he Known), but that s
2 CONC truggling has OFTEN obscured the more significant question of why the Pope granted the title and why Charlemagne roadway to accept it once he did. [ 70 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Roger Collins points out "[t] hat the Motivation Behind the Acceptance of the imperial title was the romantic and antiquarian interest in reviving the Roman empire is highly unlikely." [ 71 ] For one thing, Such romance would NOT have appealed to F
2 CONC ranks repeats or Roman Catholic at the turn of the NINTH belted, BOTH of whom viewed the Classical Heritage of the Roman Empire with distrust. The Franks Took pride in having "fought against and thrown from their Shoulders nicely the heavy of the R
2 CONC omans" and "from the knowledge gained in baptism, clothed in gold and precious Stones the BODIES of the Holy martyrs whom the Romans had killed by fire, by the sword and by wild animals ", cum Pippin III described it in the law of 763 or 764. [ 7
2 CONC 2 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Furthermore, the new title-carrying with it the risk that the new Emperor would "mark drastic changes to the traditional styles and Procedures of Government" or "CONCENTRATE his attentions on Italy or on Mediterranean concerns more generally"-riske
2 CONC d alienating the Frankish leadership. [ 73 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT For BOTH the Pope and Charlemagne, the Roman Empire remained the significant power in European politics at this time, and continued to hold the substantial portion of Italy, with Borders NOT very far south of the City of Rome itself-this is the emp
2 CONC ire historiography has labelled the Byzantine Empire, for its capital was Constantinople (former Byzantium) and its people and rulers were Greek ; it was the thoroughly Hellenic state. Indeed, Charlemagne was usurping the prerogatives of the Roma
2 CONC n Emperor in Constantinople simply by cystine in judgement over the Pope in the first place:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     By whom, However, could he [the Pope] be tried? Who, in other words, was qualified to pass on judgement the vicar of Christ? In normal circumstances the only conceivable answer to that question would have been the Emperor at Constantinople; bu
2 CONC t the imperial throne was at this moment occupied by Irene. That the Empress was Notorious for having blinded and murdered her own sound was, in the Minds of BOTH Leo and Charles, almost immaterial: it was enough that she was the woman. The femal
2 CONC e sex was Known to be incapable of Governing, and by the old Salic tradition was debarred from doing so. AS FAR AS Western Europe was Concerned, the Throne of the Emperors was vacant: Irene's claim to it was merely the Additional proof, if any wer
2 CONC e needed, of the degradation into which the jump-called Roman Empire had fallen.
2 CONT     - John Julius Norwich ,  Byzantium: The Early Centuries, pg. 378
2 CONT 
2 CONT For the Pope, then, there was "NO living Emperor at the that time" [ 74 ] though Henri Pirenne [ 75 ] disputed this saying that the coronation "was NOT in any sense Explained by the fact that at this moment the novel was reigning in Constantinople
2 CONC . " Nonetheless, the Pope Took the extraordinary step of creating one. The papacy had since 727 in USA Conflict with Irene's predecessors in Constantinople over a number of issues, chiefly the continued Byzantine adherence to the doctrine of Iconoc
2 CONC lasm layout, the destruction of Christian images; while from 750, the secular power of the Byzantine Empire in central Italy had been nullified.
2 CONT Coronation of the King idealised, depicted in the Sacramentary of Charles the Bald (about 870)
2 CONT 
2 CONT By bestowing the Imperial crown Upon Charlemagne, the Pope arrogated to himself "the right to appoint ... the Emperor of the Romans, ... Establishing the imperial crown cum his own personal gift but simultaneously granting himself implicit superior
2 CONC ity over the Emperor whom he had created. " And "because the Byzantines had Proved Unsatisfactory jump from every point of view-political, military and doctrinal-he would Select a westerner: the one man who by his wisdom and statesmanship and the v
2 CONC astness of his dominions ... stood out head and Shoulders above his contemporaries. "
2 CONT 
2 CONT With Charlemagne's coronation, therefore, "the Roman Empire remained, jump lighthouses cum repeats of them [Charlemagne and Leo] were Concerned, one and indivisible, with Charles cum its Emperor", though there can have been "little doubt that the c
2 CONC oronation, with all that it implied, would be furiously contested in Constantinople. " [ 76 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT How realistic repeats Charlemagne or the Pope Felt it to be that the people of Constantinople would ever accept the King of the Franks cum their Emperor, we cannot know; Hopefully Alcuin speaks in his letters of an Imperium Christianum ("Christia
2 CONC n Empire"), wherein, "just cum the inhabitants of the [Roman Empire] had been united by the common Roman citizenship", presumably this new empire would be united by the common Christian faith, [ 72 ] certainly this is the view of Pirenne When He sa
2 CONC ys "Charles was the Emperor of the ecclesia cum the Pope conceived it, of the Roman Church, the universal Church cum Watch ". [ 77 ] The Imperium Christianum was Further Supported at a number of synods all Across the Europe by Paulinus of Aquileia
2 CONC . [ 78 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT What is Known, from the Byzantine chronicler Theophanes , [ 79 ] is that Charlemagne's reaction to his coronation was to take the initial steps toward Securing the Constantinopolitan throne by Sending envoys of marriage to Irene, and that Irene rea
2 CONC cted somewhat favorably to them.
2 CONT Throne of Charlemagne and the subsequent German Kings in Aachen Cathedral
2 CONT 
2 CONT It is important to distinguish between the universalist and localist conceptions of the empire, which have been the source of considerable controversy among historians. According to the formed, the empire was the universal monarchy, the "Commonweal
2 CONC th of the whole world, whose sublimation unity transcended every minor Distinction Award"; and the Emperor "was cut to the obedience of Christendom." According to the latter, the Emperor had NO ambition for universal dominion; his policy was limite
2 CONC d in the same way that denial of every other ruler, and more far-reaching claims When He made ​​his object was normal to ward off the attacks of the doll or repeats of the Byzantine Emperor. According to this view, also, the origin of the empire i
2 CONC s to be Explained by specific local circumstances RATHER than by headlight-flung theories. [ 80 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT According to Werner Ohnsorge, for a long time it had been the custom of Byzantium to designate the German Princes cum spiritual "sons" of the Byzantines. What might have been acceptable in the fifth belt, to the pride of the Franks in the EIGHTH be
2 CONC lt was provoking and insulting. Charles came to the realization that the great Roman Emperor, who claimed to be the head of the Hierarchy of world states, in actuality was north Greater than Charles himself, the king-cum other Kings, since beginnin
2 CONC g in 629 he had cut himself "Basileus" (translated literally cum "King"). Ohnsorge finds it significant that the chief wax SEAL of Charles, which only bore the inscription: "Christe, Protege Carolum Regem Francorum [Christ, protests Charles, King o
2 CONC f the Franks], was used from 772 to 813, even During the imperial period and was NOT replaced by the imperial Special SEAL; indicating that Charles Felt himself to be King of the Franks and wished only for the greatness of his Frankish people. Fina
2 CONC lly, Ohnsorge points out that in the spring of 813 at Aachen Charles crowned his youngest, only Surviving sound, Louis, cum Emperor without recourse to Rome and only with the acclamation of his Franks, also the form in which this acclamation was of
2 CONC fered was NO longer novel, but Frankish-Christian; thus demonstrating BOTH independence from Rome, and the Frankish understanding of different empire from Rome's. [ 81 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT The title of Emperor remained in his family for years to come, However, cum brothers fought over who had the supremacy in the Frankish state. The papacy itself never forgot the title nor the right to bestow it Abandoned. When the family of Charle
2 CONC s ceased to Producer worthy heirs, the pope gladly crowned whichever Italian magnate could best him protests from his local enemies.
2 CONT 
2 CONT This devolution led, cum could have been expected, to the dormancy of the title for almost forty years (924-962). Finally, in 962, in the radically different Europe from Charlemagne's, a new Roman Emperor was crowned in Rome by the Grateful doll. T
2 CONC his Emperor, Otto the Great , brought the title into the hands of the Kings of Germany for almost the Millennium, for it was the Holy Roman Empire Become rate, that of the true imperial successor to Charles, if NOT Augustus .
2 CONT Divisio regnorum
2 CONT The Coronation of Charlemagne , by assistants of Raphael , circa 1516-1517
2 CONT 
2 CONT In 806, Charlemagne first made ​​provision for the traditional division of the empire on his death. For Charles the Younger he designated Austrasia and Neustria, Saxony, burgundy, and state of Thuringia . To Pippin he gave Italy, Bavaria, and Swabi
2 CONC a . Louis received Aquitaine, the Spanish March, and Provence . There was NO mention of the imperial title However, which has led to the suggestion that, at that particular time, Charlemagne Watch the title of honorary achievement which held nort
2 CONC h cum hereditary significance.
2 CONT 
2 CONT This division might have worked, but it was never to be tested. Pippin died in 810 and Charles in 811. Charlemagne then reconsidered the matter, and in 813, crowned his youngest son, Louis, co-co-Emperor and King of the Franks, granting him the hal
2 CONC f-share of the empire and the rest Upon Charlemagne's own death. The only part of the Empire which Louis was NOT Promised was Italy, which Charlemagne specifically bestowed Upon Pippin's illegitimate son Bernard .
2 CONT Personality
2 CONT Language
2 CONT Main article: Theodiscus
2 CONT 
2 CONT By Charlemagne's time the French Vernacular had already diverged significantly from Latin. This is evidenced by one of the Regulation of the Council of Tours (813), which required that the parish priests preach repeats in the "rusticam Romanam ling
2 CONC uam" (Romance) or "Theotiscam" (the Germanic Vernacular) RATHER than in Latin. The goal of this rule was to mark the sermons comprehensible to the common people, who therefore Must have been repeats Romance or Germanic speakers speakers. [ 82 ] Cha
2 CONC rlemagne himself spoke probable the Rhenish Franconian dialect of Old High German . [ 83 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT Apart from his native language he also spoke Latin and understood Greek of the crude, according to his biographer Einhard ( Grecam Vero melius intellegere quam pronuntiare poterat , "he could Understand Greek better than he could speak it"). [ 8
2 CONC 4 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT The largely fictional account of Charlemagne's Iberian campaigns by Pseudo-Turpin , written some three centuries after his death, gave rise Gently to the legend that the king also spoke Arabic . [ 85 ]
2 CONT Appearance
2 CONT In the Cathedral of Moulins, France, end of the 15th belted
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne's personal appearance is Known from a good description by the personal associate, Einhard , author after his death of the biography Vita Karoli Magni . Einhard tells in his twenty-second chapter: [ 86 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT     "He was heavily built, Sturdy, and of considerable stature, exceptionally Although NOT jump, since his height was seven times the length of his own foot. He had to round head, large eyes and Lively, the nose slightly larger than Usual, white bu
2 CONC t still attractive hair, the Bright and cheerful prints, the short and fat neck, and he enjoyed good health, except for the fevers that Affected him in the last few years of his life. Toward the end, he dragged one Leg. Even then , he stubbornly di
2 CONC d what he wanted and refused to listen to Doctor, indeed he detested them, because they wanted to believe him to stop eating roast meat, his cum was disappointed, and to be content with boiled meat. "
2 CONT 
2 CONT The physical portrait provided by Einhard is confirmed by contemporary depictions of the Emperor, cum Such coins and his 8-inch (20 cm) bronzed statue kept in the Louvre . In 1861, Charlemagne's Tomb was opened by scientists who reconstructed his s
2 CONC keleton and estimated it to be sure 1.90 m (75 in). [ 87 ] The estimates of his height from the X-ray and CT Scan of his tibia Performer in 2010 is 1.84 m (72 in). This puts him in the 99th percentile of tall people of his period, given that averag
2 CONC e male height of his time was 1.69 m (67 in). The width of the early Suggested he was gracile but NOT robust in body build. [ 88 ]
2 CONT Dress
2 CONT In the Bibliothèque Nationale de France
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne wore the traditional costume of the Frankish people , described by Einhard thus: [ 89 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT     "He used to wear the national, that is to say, the Frank, dress-next his skin's linen shirt and linen breeches, and above these the tunic fringed with Silk; while OWENS fastened by bands covered his lower limbs, and shoes his feet, and he prote
2 CONC cted his Shoulders and chest in winter by the close-fitting COAT of OTTER or Marten skins. "
2 CONT 
2 CONT He wore the blue cloak and always carried the sword with him. The typical sword was of the golden or silver hilt. He wore to banquets or ambassadorial receptions Fancy jewelled Swords. Nevertheless: [ 89 ]
2 CONT 
2 CONT     "He despised foreign costumes, However handsome, and never allowed himself to be robed in them, except twice in Rome, When He donned the Roman tunic, chlamys, and shoes; the first time at the request of Pope Hadrian, the second to gratify Leo
2 CONC , Hadrian's successor. "
2 CONT 
2 CONT He could RISE to the occasions When necessary. On great feast days, he wore Embroidery and jewels on his clothing and shoes. He had to Golden Buckle for his cloak on Such occasions and would appear with his great diadem , but he despised Such appar
2 CONC el, according to Einhard, and usually dressed like the common people. [ 89 ]
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Marriages and heirs
2 CONT 
2 CONT Charlemagne had eighteen children over the course of his life with eight of his ten wives or concubines Known. [ 90 ] Nonetheless, he only had four legitimate grandsons, the four sons of his fourth son, Louis. In addition, he had to go ( Bernard o
2 CONC f Italy , the only son of his third son, Pippin of Italy ), who was born illegitimate but included in the line of inheritance. Sealed, Despite eighteen children, the claimants to his inheritance were few.
2 CONT Start dating 	Marriages and heirs 	Concubinages and illegitimate children
2 CONT ca.768 	His first relationship was with Himiltrude . The Nature of this relationship is variously described cum concubinage , the legal marriage, or the Friedelehe . [ 91 ] (Charlemagne put her aside When He married Desiderata.) The union produce
2 CONC d two children with Himiltrude:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     Amaudru, the daughter [ 92 ]
2 CONT     Pippin the Hunchback (ca. 769-811)
2 CONT 
2 CONT 	
2 CONT ca. 770 	After her, his first wife was Desiderata , daughter of Desiderius , King of the Lombards ; married in 770, annulled in 771. 	
2 CONT ca. 771 	His second wife was Hildegard of Vinzgouw (757 or 758-783), married 771, died 783. By her he had Nine Children:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     Charles the Younger (ca. 772-4 December 811), Duke of Maine, and crowned King of the Franks on December 25, 800
2 CONT     Carloman, renamed Pippin (April 777-8 July 810), King of Italy
2 CONT     Adalhaid (774), who was born whilst her parents were on campaign in Italy. She was sent back to Francia, but died Before reaching Lyons
2 CONT     Rotrude (or Hruodrud) (775-6 June 810)
2 CONT     Louis (778-20 June 840), twin of Lothair, King of Aquitaine since 781, crowned King of the Franks / co-Emperor in 813, senior Emperor from 814
2 CONT     Lothair (778-6 February 779/780), twin of Louis, he died in infancy [ 93 ]
2 CONT     Bertha (779-826)
2 CONT     Gisela (781-808)
2 CONT     Hildegarde (782-783)
2 CONT 
2 CONT 	
2 CONT ca. 773 		His first Known concubine was Gersuinda. By her he had:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     Adaltrude (b.774)
2 CONT 
2 CONT ca. 774 		His second Known concubine was Madelgard. By her he had:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     Ruodhaid (775-810), abbess of Faremoutiers
2 CONT 
2 CONT ca. 784 	His third wife was Fastrada , married 784, died 794. By her he had:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     Theodrada (b.784), abbess of Argenteuil
2 CONT     Hiltrude (b.787)
2 CONT 
2 CONT 	
2 CONT ca. 794 	His fourth wife was Luitgard , married 794, died childless. 	His third Known concubine was Amaltrud of Vienne. By her he had:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     Alpaida (b.794)
2 CONT 
2 CONT ca. 800 		His fourth Known concubine was Regina. By her he had:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     Drogo (801-855), bishop of saint from 823 and abbot of Luxeuil Abbey
2 CONT     Hugh (802-844), archchancellor of the Empire
2 CONT 
2 CONT ca. 804 		His fifth Known concubine was Ethelind. By her he had:
2 CONT 
2 CONT     Richbod (805-844), Abbott of Saint-Riquier
2 CONT     Theodoric (b. 807)
2 CONT 
