2 CONC hen was attacked again, this time by&amp;nbsp;Odo of Champagnewho attacked the imperial palace while&amp;nbsp;Conrad&amp;nbsp;was absent. He relinquished it quickly and was killed soon thereafter.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998
2 CONC 092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[25]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;The palace and town of Aachen received fortifying walls by order of&amp;nbsp;Emperor Frederick Barbarossa&amp;nbsp;between 1172 and 1176.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size
2 CONC : 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[17]&lt;/sup&gt;Over the next 500 years, most&amp;nbsp;kings of Germany&amp;nbsp;destined to reign over the&amp;nbsp;Holy Roman Empire&amp;nbsp;were crowned in Aachen.Charles IV&amp;nbsp;wa
2 CONC s not crowned in Aachen, but in&amp;nbsp;Bonn, after his father&amp;nbsp;John&amp;nbsp;died in battle, due to Aachen siding with&amp;nbsp;Ludwig, in a dispute dating back twenty years.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; un
2 CONC icode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[26]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;The original audience hall built by Charlemagne was torn down and replaced by the current&amp;nbsp;city hall&amp;nbsp;in 1330.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; u
