2 CONC -height: inherit;"&gt;By the middle of the 19th century, industrialisation swept away most of the city's medieval rules of production and commerce, although the entirely corrupt remains of the city's medieval constitution were kept in place (compar
2 CONC e the famous remarks of Georg Forster in his&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Ansichten vom Niederrhein&lt;/em&gt;) until 1801, when Aachen became the "chef-lieu&amp;nbsp;du&amp;nbsp;d&amp;eacute;partement de la Roer" in Napoleon's&amp;nbsp;First French Empire
2 CONC . In 1815 after the&amp;nbsp;Napoleonic Wars, the&amp;nbsp;Kingdom of Prussia&amp;nbsp;took over and the city became one of its most socially and politically backward centres until the end of the 19th century.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-siz
2 CONC e: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[4]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;Administered within the&amp;nbsp;Rhine Province, by 1880 the population was 80,000. Starting in 1838, the railway from&amp;nbsp;Cologne&amp;nbsp;to&amp;nbsp;Belgium&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;passed through Aachen.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[36]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;The city suffered extreme overcrowding and deplorable sanitary conditions up to 1875 when th
2 CONC e medieval fortifications were finally abandoned as a limit to building operations and new, less miserable quarters were built in the eastern part of the city, where drainage of waste liquids was easiest. In December 1880, the&amp;nbsp;Aachen tramw
2 CONC ay networkwas opened, and in 1895 it was electrified.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[37]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;In the 19th century and up to the 1930s, the city was important for th
2 CONC e production of railway locomotives and carriages, iron,&amp;nbsp;pins,&amp;nbsp;needles,&amp;nbsp;buttons, tobacco, woollen goods, and silk goods.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="20th_century" class="mw-headline"&gt;20th century&lt;/span&gt;&lt
