2 CONC g/w/index.php?title=Aachen&amp;amp;action=edit&amp;amp;section=9"&gt;edit&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span class="mw-editsection-bracket" style="margin-left: 0px; color: #555555; margin-right: 0px;"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin
2 CONC : 0.5em 0px; line-height: inherit;"&gt;After&amp;nbsp;World War I, Aachen was occupied by the Allies until 1930.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[27]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;Aachen was on
2 CONC e of the locations involved in the ill-fatedRhenish Republic. On 21 October 1923 an armed band took over city hall. Similar actions took place in&amp;nbsp;Munchen-Gladbach,Duisburg, and&amp;nbsp;Krefeld. This Republic lasted only about a year.&lt;s
2 CONC up style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[38]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;Aachen was heavily damaged during&amp;nbsp;World War II. The city and its fortified surroundings were laid siege to from 12 Septem
2 CONC ber&amp;ndash;21 October 1944 by the US 1st Infantry Division&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[39]&lt;/sup&gt;with the 3rd Armored Division assisting from the south.&lt;sup style="line
2 CONC -height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[40]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;Around 13 October the US 2nd Armored Division played their part, coming from the north and getting as close as&amp;nbsp;Wuerselen,&lt;sup style=
2 CONC "line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[41]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;while the 30th Infantry Division played a crucial role in completing the encirclement of Aachen on 16 October 1944.&lt;sup style="line-heig
2 CONC ht: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[42]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;With reinforcements from the US 28th Infantry Division&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&g
2 CONC t;[43]&lt;/sup&gt;the&amp;nbsp;Battle of Aachen&amp;nbsp;then continued involving direct assaults through the heavily defended city, which finally forced the German garrison to surrender on 21 October 1944.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size
2 CONC : 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[39]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;Aachen was the first German city to be captured by the Allies, and its residents welcomed the soldiers as liberators.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.199
2 CONC 9998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[44]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;The city was destroyed partially &amp;ndash; and in some parts completely &amp;ndash; during the fighting,&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unico
2 CONC de-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[4]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;mostly by&amp;nbsp;American&amp;nbsp;artillery&amp;nbsp;fire and demolitions carried out by the&amp;nbsp;Waffen-SS&amp;nbsp;defenders. Damaged buildings included the medieval churches of&amp;nb
2 CONC sp;St. Foillan,&amp;nbsp;St. Paul&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;St. Nicholas, and the&amp;nbsp;Rathaus&amp;nbsp;(city hall), although&amp;nbsp;Aachen Cathedral&amp;nbsp;was largely unscathed. Only 4,000 inhabitants remained in the city; the rest had follow
2 CONC ed evacuation orders. Its first Allied-appointed mayor,&amp;nbsp;Franz Oppenhoff, was assassinated by an SS commando unit.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="History_of_Aachen_Jews" class="mw-headline"&gt;History of Aachen Jews&lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n class="mw-editsection" style="-webkit-user-select: none; font-size: small; font-weight: normal; margin-left: 1em; vertical-align: baseline; line-height: 1em; display: inline-block; white-space: nowrap; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n class="mw-editsection-bracket" style="margin-right: 0px; color: #555555; margin-left: 0px;"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;a style="text-decoration: none; color: #0b0080; background: none;" title="Edit section: History of Aachen Jews" href="https://en.wiki
2 CONC pedia.org/w/index.php?title=Aachen&amp;amp;action=edit&amp;amp;section=10"&gt;edit&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span class="mw-editsection-bracket" style="margin-left: 0px; color: #555555; margin-right: 0px;"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style=
2 CONC "margin: 0.5em 0px; line-height: inherit;"&gt;During the&amp;nbsp;Roman&amp;nbsp;period, Aachen was the site of a flourishing&amp;nbsp;Jewish&amp;nbsp;community. Later, during the&amp;nbsp;Carolingian&amp;nbsp;empire, aJewish&amp;nbsp;community wa
2 CONC s found near the royal palace.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[16]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;In 802, a Jew named Isaac accompanied the ambassador ofCharlemagne&amp;nbsp;to&amp;nbsp;Harun a
2 CONC l-Rashid. During the 13th Century, many&amp;nbsp;Jews&amp;nbsp;converted to Christianity, as shown in the records of the Church of St. Marry. In 1486, the&amp;nbsp;Jews&amp;nbsp;of Aachen offered gifts to Maximilian the first during his coronatio
2 CONC n ceremony. In 1629, the Aachen&amp;nbsp;Jewish&amp;nbsp;community was expelled from the city. In 1667, six&amp;nbsp;Jews&amp;nbsp;were allowed to return. Most of the Aachen&amp;nbsp;Jews&amp;nbsp;settled in the nearby town of&amp;nbsp;Burtscheid
2 CONC . On 16 May 1815, the&amp;nbsp;Jewish&amp;nbsp;community of the city offered an homage in its synagogue to the Prussian king, Friedrich Wilhelm the third. A&amp;nbsp;Jewish&amp;nbsp;cemetery was acquired in 1851. 1,345Jews&amp;nbsp;lived in the cit
2 CONC y in 1933. The synagogue was destroyed during&amp;nbsp;Kristallnacht&amp;nbsp;in 1938. In 1939, after emigration and arrests, 782&amp;nbsp;Jews&amp;nbsp;remained in the city. After&amp;nbsp;World War II, only 62&amp;nbsp;Jews&amp;nbsp;lived there
2 CONC . In 2003, 1,434&amp;nbsp;Jews&amp;nbsp;were living in Aachen, occasionally suffering of antisemitic incidents at its community facilities.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[45]&lt;/sup
2 CONC &gt;&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[46]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;In&amp;nbsp;Jewish&amp;nbsp;texts, the city of Aachen was called Aish, or Ash (אש).&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="21
