2 CONC <line>&lt;p style="margin: 0.5em 0px; line-height: inherit;"&gt;Aachen Cathedral&amp;nbsp;was erected on the orders of&amp;nbsp;Charlemagne&amp;nbsp;in AD 796&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolat
2 CONC e;"&gt;[5]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;and was, on completion, in 798,&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[57]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;the largest cathedral north of the&amp;nbsp;Alps. It was model
2 CONC ed after the&amp;nbsp;Basilica of San Vitale, in&amp;nbsp;Ravenna, Italy,&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[27]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;and was built by&amp;nbsp;Odo of Metz.&lt;sup style=
2 CONC "line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[5]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;Charlemagne also desired for the chapel to compete with the&amp;nbsp;Lateran Palace, both in quality and authority.&lt;sup style="line-heigh
2 CONC t: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[58]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;It was originally built in the&amp;nbsp;Carolingianstyle, including marble covered walls, and mosaic inlay on the dome.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1
2 CONC ; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[59]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;On his death, Charlemagne's remains were interred in the cathedral and can be seen there to this day. The cathedral was extended several times in later ag
2 CONC es, turning it into a curious and unique mixture of building styles. The throne and gallery portion date from the&amp;nbsp;Ottonian, with portions of the original&amp;nbsp;opus sectile&amp;nbsp;floor still visible.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; fon
2 CONC t-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[59]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;The 13th century saw gables being added to the roof, and after the fire of 1656, the dome was rebuilt. Finally, a&amp;nbsp;choir&amp;nbsp;was added around th
2 CONC e start of the 15th century&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[22]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;After,&amp;nbsp;Frederick Barbarossa&amp;nbsp;canonizedCharlemagne, in 1165, the chapel became a d
2 CONC estination for pilgrims.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[22]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;For 600 years, from 936 to 1531, Aachen Cathedral was the church of coronation for 30 German kings an
2 CONC d 12 queens. The church built by Charlemagne is still the main attraction of the city.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[60]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;In addition to holding the remains of i
2 CONC ts founder, it became the burial place of his successor&amp;nbsp;Otto III. In the upper chamber of the gallery, Charlemagne's marble throne is housed.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[
2 CONC 61]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;Aachen Cathedral has been designated as a&amp;nbsp;UNESCO&amp;nbsp;World Heritage Site.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0.5em 0px; line-height: inherit;"&gt;Most of the marble and columns used in the constructio
2 CONC n of the cathedral were brought from&amp;nbsp;Rome&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;Ravenna, including the&amp;nbsp;sarcophagus&amp;nbsp;that Charlemagne was eventually laid to rest.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -
2 CONC webkit-isolate;"&gt;[58]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;A bronze bear from&amp;nbsp;Gaul&amp;nbsp;was placed inside, along with an equestrian statue from Ravenna, believed to be&amp;nbsp;Theodric. These were in contrast to a wolf and a statue of&amp;nbsp;Mar
2 CONC cus Aureliusin the&amp;nbsp;Capitoline.&lt;sup style="line-height: 1; font-size: 11.1999998092651px; unicode-bidi: -webkit-isolate;"&gt;[58]&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;Bronze pieces such as the doors and railings were cast in a local foundry, some of whi
2 CONC ch have survived to the present. Finally, there is uncertainty surrounding the bronze pine cone in the chapel, and where it was created. Wherever it was made, it was also a parallel to a piece in Rome, this in&amp;nbsp;Old St. Peter's Basilica.&lt;
