2 TITL CHARLES DE SAINT-&#201;TIENNE DE LA TOUR
1 _OID 46702b42-f274-464b-9ff3-207f17c4441e
1 _DSCR Source: http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/saint_etienne_de_la_tour_charles_de_1593_1666_1E.html
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SAINT-&amp;Eacute;TIENNE DE LA TOUR, CHARLES DE [&lt;/strong&gt;the family name may well have been &lt;strong&gt;Turgis]&lt;/strong&gt;, trader, colonizer, and governor of Acadia; b. 1593, probabl
2 CONC y in the old French province of Champagne, the son of C&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;laude de &lt;/span&gt;S&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;aint&lt;/span&gt;-&amp;Eacute;tienne de La&amp;nbsp;Tour; d.&amp;nbsp;1666.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;O
2 CONC n 25&amp;nbsp;Feb.&amp;nbsp;1610 Charles set sail from Dieppe for Acadia with his father and a party of men led by J&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;ean de &lt;/span&gt;B&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;iencourt &lt;/span&gt;de Poutrincourt to reoccupy t
2 CONC he abandoned settlement at Port-Royal (now Annapolis Royal, N.S.). During the years that followed, affairs in Acadia were most often in charge of Poutrincourt&amp;rsquo;s son, C&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;harles de &lt;/span&gt;B&lt;span class="S
2 CONC mallCaps"&gt;iencourt&lt;/span&gt;. He and young La&amp;nbsp;Tour were close friends, with La&amp;nbsp;Tour serving as his lieutenant.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Following the destruction of Port-Royal by Samuel Argall in 1613, Biencourt and hi
2 CONC s men were forced to live with the Indians for a time. With little in the way of supplies and reinforcements arriving from France, Biencourt and La&amp;nbsp;Tour were able to rebuild the Port-Royal buildings only in part. They largely abandoned set
2 CONC tlement and farming in favour of the lucrative but risky fur trade, a commerce which each year attracted several vessels to the Acadian coasts. When Biencourt died in 1623, he left Charles de La&amp;nbsp;Tour as his heir. La&amp;nbsp;Tour took char
2 CONC ge of the colony and soon after he built up a strong post at Cap de Sable, called Fort Lomeron in honour of David Lomeron who was his agent in France. Here he carried on a sizeable trade in furs with the Indians and farmed the land.&lt;/p&gt;</line
2 CONC ><line>&lt;p&gt;When La&amp;nbsp;Tour learned of the outbreak of war between France and England in 1627, he was concerned for the future. For the past 20 years the French government had largely ignored Acadia, leaving it without defence or economi
2 CONC c aid. The last remaining post France had in the country was La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s Fort Lomeron. He fully realized something had to be done quickly to strengthen defences if Acadia were to remain French, and he wrote letters both to King&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;Louis and to Cardinal&amp;nbsp;Richelieu pointing out the lack of support and the fact, that, exercising the authority of administrator in Acadia as the heir of Biencourt, he had maintained the hold France had on Acadia. He went on to repor
2 CONC t that he had trained a mixed force of Frenchmen and Indians which he had used to prevent English attempts to trade and to fish in Acadia. He ended with a request for supplies and reinforcements as well as for a proper commission authorizing him t
2 CONC o defend the area. La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s father presented the letters and was able to give a full account of conditions in Acadia.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The request for supplies and men was turned over to the powerful new trading as
2 CONC sociation&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; the Compagnie de la Nouvelle-France (called also the Compagnie des Cent-Associ&amp;eacute;s)&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; formed that same year, 1627, and given the right to trade with the Indians and to grant seigneuries whi
2 CONC le encouraging the settlement of New France. The next spring the company sent out under R&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;oquemont &lt;/span&gt;de Brison four ships laden with supplies for Quebec and Cap de Sable. Three ships, commanded by Sir&amp;nbs
2 CONC p;D&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;avid &lt;/span&gt;Kirke, which were accompanying some vessels carrying W&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;illiam &lt;/span&gt;Alexander the younger and his settlers, captured the French ships (and Claude de La&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC Tour). As a result, no help got through to Charles. With the taking of Quebec by the English in 1629, the sole French stronghold left in New France was La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s Fort Lomeron.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;After his capture, Cla
2 CONC ude de La&amp;nbsp;Tour espoused the English cause and promised to win over his son, in exchange for a large grant of land in &amp;ldquo;New Scotland.&amp;rdquo; The elder La&amp;nbsp;Tour set out immediately afterwards with two British warships ca
2 CONC rrying colonists and soldiers to the Anglo-Scottish settlement at Port-Royal. A stop was made at Cap de Sable and Claude hastened to inform his son of all that had taken place. He urged Charles to give up this last possession of France in Acadia an
2 CONC d to accept the title and generous land-grant being offered him by the English. The emphatic reaction of the younger La&amp;nbsp;Tour, as Champlain tells us, was that &amp;ldquo;he would rather have died than consent to such baseness as to betray h
2 CONC is King.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Since his urging and pleading were in vain, Claude resorted to force. For over 24 hours troops presumably led by Claude de La&amp;nbsp;Tour attacked Fort Lomeron, but without success. Charles, then
2 CONC , was victorious in the cause of France, while his uncomfortable father was obliged to retire with the English to Port-Royal,&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Shortly afterwards, two vessels of the Compagnie de la Nouvelle-France, sent by Jean Tuffet
2 CONC , arrived at Fort Lomeron with supplies and a relief party including workmen, artisans, and three Recollet priests. Bernard M&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;arot&lt;/span&gt;, the leader of the expedition, brought with him letters from the company ap
2 CONC pointing La&amp;nbsp;Tour one of its associates and explaining that the food, arms, and men had been sent to enable him to build a habitation where he felt it would be most useful. The workmen were soon set to the task of enlarging and strengthenin
2 CONC g the post at Cap de Sable, and apparently it was renamed Fort La&amp;nbsp;Tour (although Champlain called it &amp;ldquo;Fort sainct Louys&amp;rdquo; in his Works (Biggar), VI, 199). At the same time, Charles consulted with Marot and the Recollet
2 CONC s about the problem of his father and then decided to allow Claude to return to Cap de Sable.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Because of the new interest shown by France in Acadia, La&amp;nbsp;Tour resolved to build a fortified trading post at the m
2 CONC outh of the Saint John River, the richest source of furs in all Acadia. He reasoned that this would forestall any designs the English might have on that part of the country. La&amp;nbsp;Tour immediately sent to France an emissary, Krainguille, fo
2 CONC r the additional men and supplies required for the project. On 8&amp;nbsp;Feb.&amp;nbsp;1631 Louis&amp;nbsp;XIII signed a royal commission naming Charles de La&amp;nbsp;Tour governor and lieutenant-general of the king; it was delivered either by La
2 CONC urent Ferchaud or Krainguille, who brought out the requested materials for La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s use at Saint John. When the post there was completed, it was named Fort Sainte-Marie and Jean-Daniel Chaline, one of La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;
2 CONC s lieutenants, was placed in charge of this first permanent establishment on the Saint John. On 18&amp;nbsp;Sept.&amp;nbsp;1632, a force of Scots from Port-Royal under command of Capt.&amp;nbsp;Andrew F&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;orrester &lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;attacked the new fort, tore down a large cross, damaged the chapel, and plundered the supplies. Not too many months elapsed before La&amp;nbsp;Tour captured the English fort at Machias and pillaged it as a warning that his posts could not b
2 CONC e molested with impunity.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;With the signing of the Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye in 1632, France recovered Acadia and Canada. Isaac de R&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;azilly &lt;/span&gt;was given a commission as gove
2 CONC rnor in Acadia and ordered to take possession of it. He arrived at La&amp;nbsp;H&amp;egrave;ve that fall and a few months later secured the return of Port-Royal from the Scots. With the country once more under French control, La&amp;nbsp;Tour wen
2 CONC t to France where he had the limits of his authority and that of Razilly clarified by the company: under this arrangement, Razilly was given control of La&amp;nbsp;H&amp;egrave;ve, Port-Royal, and the Sainte-Croix area. While in France La&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC Tour also recruited colonists for his headquarters, which he moved to Fort Sainte-Marie in 1635. Everything indicates that La&amp;nbsp;Tour got on well with Razilly and that under their direction trade flourished and settlers were attracted to th
2 CONC e land. Just as conditions in Acadia were undergoing such an encouraging change, Isaac de Razilly died suddenly in 1635 and a period of confusion and strife began.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Claude de Rasilly succeeded his brother. Since he fou
2 CONC nd it necessary to stay in France, he delegated the task of looking after the Rasilly interests in Acadia to his cousin, Charles de M&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;enou &lt;/span&gt;d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay. It was not long before d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay and L
2 CONC a&amp;nbsp;Tour, both energetic and ambitious men with indefinite areas of command, came into conflict. The story of Acadia for the next decade is largely a record of their strife, which worsened to armed conflict and paralysed colonization. This d
2 CONC eplorable clash between noblemen of the same race, religion, and allegiance had the result of obscuring the history of the period to a surprising degree. What is more, the smoke-screen of prejudice, both contemporary and modern, has made it difficu
2 CONC lt to determine the facts that should be the basis of unprejudiced judgement by the historian. Happily, enough documentation remains to allow us to trace with a fair degree of accuracy the remarkable feud between La&amp;nbsp;Tour and d&amp;rsquo;Au
2 CONC lnay.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;In 1636, the Compagnie de la Nouvelle-France gave a trading post at Pentagouet (on the Penobscot) to Claude de La&amp;nbsp;Tour. Built possibly about 1625 by the elder La&amp;nbsp;Tour, the post had been capture
2 CONC d by the New Englanders in 1626, then retaken by d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay in 1635. The company&amp;rsquo;s action seems to have roused d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay&amp;rsquo;s hatred for both La&amp;nbsp;Tour and the company. D&amp;rsquo;Aulnay addressed himself d
2 CONC irectly to the king who in 1638 gave Pentagouet to d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay and made an unsuccessful attempt to divide all of Acadia west of Canseau (Canso) between them.&amp;nbsp;In their ignorance, Louis&amp;nbsp;XIII&amp;rsquo;s ministers gave d&amp;r
2 CONC squo;Aulnay the land lying north of the Bay of Fundy but not Fort Sainte-Marie, and La&amp;nbsp;Tour the peninsular part of Acadia but not Port-Royal. This simply made matters worse and the struggle continued.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Under a
2 CONC n arrangement instituted by the company some years before, Razilly and La&amp;nbsp;Tour shared the expenses and the profits of the fur trade. However, when La&amp;nbsp;Tour went to Port-Royal in 1640 to check the furs and supplies there, he was ref
2 CONC used permission and it appears that he and d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay came to blows. The latter complained to the king about what he termed this act of aggression and was directed to carry orders to La&amp;nbsp;Tour asking the latter to return to France an
2 CONC d explain his conduct. This La&amp;nbsp;Tour bluntly refused to do, on the grounds that the order was obtained through misrepresentation. Using this to best advantage, d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay was able gradually to improve his position at court. Under in
2 CONC structions from the king to put Fort La&amp;nbsp;Tour in the hands of &amp;ldquo;faithful personages,&amp;rdquo; he captured and burned the post, keeping all the goods he found there for himself.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;When he learned tha
2 CONC t La&amp;nbsp;Tour, cut off from trade goods and other supplies from France, had sent his lieutenant G&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;argot &lt;/span&gt;to Boston asking for the right to trade and to recruit mercenaries, d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay hastened t
2 CONC o France, to claim that La&amp;nbsp;Tour had committed treason, and that he had shown disrespect for the Crown by refusing to face the king. In August of 1642 he returned to Acadia with orders for La&amp;nbsp;Tour to appear before the king to answe
2 CONC r these serious charges.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;La&amp;nbsp;Tour and his wife, Fran&amp;ccedil;oise-Marie Jacquelin, decided it was best that she go to France and that he stay in Acadia to protect his interests. Once in France, Mme de La&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;Tour won from the vice-admiral authorization for the Compagnie de la Nouvelle-France to send a vessel with soldiers and supplies to La&amp;nbsp;Tour. Sailing in April&amp;nbsp;1643, it entered the Baie Fran&amp;ccedil;aise (Bay of Fundy) t
2 CONC o find that d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay had established a three-ship blockade of the Saint John post. Since the relief ship was not able to get through, La&amp;nbsp;Tour managed to reach her under cover of darkness and to persuade the captain to take him t
2 CONC o Boston. There La&amp;nbsp;Tour showed his authorization from the vice-admiral of France to receive supplies that were now illegally denied him by d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay, and he received permission to obtain private assistance. By mortgaging his prope
2 CONC rty to Major-General&amp;nbsp;Edward Gibbons of Boston he was able to hire four ships and a number of soldiers.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;On sighting this fleet in the Bay of Fundy, d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay fled to Port-Royal where he was pursued b
2 CONC y La&amp;nbsp;Tour. There La&amp;nbsp;Tour wished to discuss the matter of compensation for his losses but d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay refused to negotiate. La&amp;nbsp;Tour then attacked with a force of his own men&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; augmented by a grou
2 CONC p of 30 English volunteers&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; defeated d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay&amp;rsquo;s force, and burned the mill. D&amp;rsquo;Aulnay was not slow in lodging a complaint with the Crown, charging La&amp;nbsp;Tour with open rebellion and having turn
2 CONC ed traitor in allying himself with the English for the purpose of driving the French from Acadia. The result was that La&amp;nbsp;Tour was totally discredited with the French government. Soon after, when Mme La&amp;nbsp;Tour arrived in France onc
2 CONC e more to obtain the supplies her husband desperately needed, she was even refused permission to leave the country and had to flee in disguise to England.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Having won the complete favour of the king, d&amp;rsquo;Aulna
2 CONC y hired soldiers, purchased both a warship and ammunition, and arrived at Port-Royal in September&amp;nbsp;1644. At this time he signed a treaty of peace with the English of Boston. There is no doubt that he as well as La&amp;nbsp;Tour had been car
2 CONC rying on trade with the New Englanders, who preferred to deal with La&amp;nbsp;Tour but strove to be impartial because of their fear of d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay. D&amp;rsquo;Aulnay then sent a representative to Boston asking for aid in his struggle wit
2 CONC h La&amp;nbsp;Tour. Aid was refused and the suggestion made that he seek instead to make peace with his rival. Such a proposal fell on deaf ears. Victorious at court, he was determined to be victor in the field as well.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&
2 CONC gt;During La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s absence from the Saint John early in February&amp;nbsp;1645, d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay attacked Fort Sainte-Marie but was beaten off with heavy loss. When in April he learned from deserters that La&amp;nbsp;Tour wa
2 CONC s once more away at Boston in search of food and trade goods, d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay resolved to attack again. This time he was successful, but more through deception than force of arms. News of the capture of his fort and of his wife&amp;rsquo;s deat
2 CONC h reached La&amp;nbsp;Tour at Boston while he was still preparing his relief expedition.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;The next year Charles moved to Quebec where he was warmly welcomed by Governor H&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;uault &lt;/span&gt
2 CONC ;de Montmagny who gave him lodging at the Ch&amp;acirc;teau Saint-Louis. During the four years that followed he was busily occupied in trade, assisted the Jesuits in their missionary efforts, and on at least one occasion fought with the Hurons agai
2 CONC nst the Iroquois. When news of d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay&amp;rsquo;s death in 1650 reached Quebec, La&amp;nbsp;Tour decided at last to go to France to plead his case. There, he begged that an inquiry be made into his conduct as well as into the faults bot
2 CONC h he and the Compagnie de la Nouvelle-France had found in d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay&amp;rsquo;s conduct. The inquiry was granted. It resulted in La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s being re-established in the royal favour; responsibility for the bloody fightin
2 CONC g between the two was charged to d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;With his property and his commission as governor restored, La&amp;nbsp;Tour gathered several families of colonists, including that of his childhood friend M&lt;spa
2 CONC n class="SmallCaps"&gt;ius &lt;/span&gt;d&amp;rsquo;Entremont, and sailed in the summer of 1653 for Port-Royal. He presented Mme d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay [Jeanne M&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;otin&lt;/span&gt;] with a royal order restoring to him the fo
2 CONC rt at Saint John. Mme d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay and La&amp;nbsp;Tour were both heavily in debt and anxious to bring the disastrous rivalry of their two factions to an end, and so, in due course, she accepted La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s proposal of marria
2 CONC ge. Later that same summer E&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;mmanuel &lt;/span&gt;L&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Borgne, to whom the d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay estate &lt;span&gt;owed over 200,000 &lt;em&gt;livres&lt;/em&gt;,&lt;/span&
2 CONC gt; arrived at Port-Royal to collect the debt. He discovered La&amp;nbsp;Tour was absent at Fort Sainte-Marie and took the opportunity to make off with all the pelts he could find. The next year Le&amp;nbsp;Borgne made an attempt to take the fort a
2 CONC t Saint John, which was unsuccessful since La&amp;nbsp;Tour had been forewarned by N&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;icolas &lt;/span&gt;D&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;enys&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;On 14&amp;nbsp;July&amp;nbsp;1654
2 CONC , an English expedition sent by Oliver Cromwell under the command of Major&amp;nbsp;Robert S&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;edgwick &lt;/span&gt;entered the harbour at Saint John and called for La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s surrender. Having few canno
2 CONC n, almost no ammunition, and a garrison of but 70 men to oppose a force of 500, La&amp;nbsp;Tour was obliged to comply. He was made a prisoner and taken to England. Only in 1656 was he allowed to see Cromwell. He asked for the return of his propert
2 CONC y on the grounds that England and France had been at peace when the capture took place. Cromwell refused the request, agreeing only to recognize La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s right as a baronet of Nova Scotia as his father&amp;rsquo;s heir, provide
2 CONC d he accepted English allegiance and paid both the amount he owed Boston merchants and the cost of the English garrison that L&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;everett &lt;/span&gt;had maintained at Saint John. Discouraged with the turn of events and u
2 CONC ndoubtedly dispirited at the thought of the French creditors awaiting him and his wife, Charles accepted these conditions. (It is of interest to note that in 1700 the king of France indicated an understanding of this action when he recognized the r
2 CONC ights in Acadia of La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s children.) To raise the some &amp;pound;15,000 involved, La&amp;nbsp;Tour entered into partnership with William Crowne and Thomas Temple.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Soon afterwards&amp;nbsp;&amp;n
2 CONC dash; in September, 1656&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; La&amp;nbsp;Tour, possibly finding this enforced arrangement with the English distasteful, sold his rights to his two partners, retaining only a small percentage of the profit. Temple took command of th
2 CONC e fort at Saint John and Crowne that at Pentagouet. La&amp;nbsp;Tour appears to have retired with his wife to Cap de Sable and there he died in 1666. In all, he had been a resident of Acadia for 56 years and his is the name that predominates durin
2 CONC g most of that period.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Charles de La&amp;nbsp;Tour was married three times. The first marriage, to an unnamed Micmac girl, was blessed in 1626. By this union he had three daughters, two of whom entered religious order
2 CONC s and the third, Jeanne, who later married Martin d&amp;rsquo;A&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;prendestiguy &lt;/span&gt;de Martignon. La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s second marriage, to the valiant Fran&amp;ccedil;oise-Marie Jacquelin, took place at Po
2 CONC rt-Royal in 1640. They had one son who apparently died during childhood. La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s third wife was d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay&amp;rsquo;s widow, Jeanne Motin, whom he married at Port-Royal in 1653; they had five children.&lt;/p&gt;</line>
2 CONC <line>&lt;p&gt;There is little doubt that La&amp;nbsp;Tour remained loyal to his country until 1656, all of his previous dealings with the English occurring in peace-time and being a recognized practice. Unquestionably, La&amp;nbsp;Tour would hav
2 CONC e preferred to obtain the goods he required in France, but the acts of his rival had the very effect of forcing him more and more to turn to Boston for supplies. In fact, we may say that in the face of an all too often uninterested and short-sighte
2 CONC d government he went to unusual lengths to preserve French power, although he was only a trader and was not really interested in colonization.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;So far as his ruinous struggle with d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay is concerned, it i
2 CONC s usually thought that La&amp;nbsp;Tour struck the first physical blow, but there is no proof of this. Had he been willing to conciliate his vindictive enemy, the fratricidal battle might not have reached such heights.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&g
2 CONC t;Charles de La&amp;nbsp;Tour, who must remain something of a controversial figure, was ambitious, confident of his own judgement, and the possessor of great natural ability and determination. He was a born leader with the happy faculty of making f
2 CONC riends and of inspiring faith in his integrity. His associations with the French court, Huault de Montmagny, Boston merchants, and d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay&amp;rsquo;s widow&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; if not with d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; testif
2 CONC y to his diplomatic persuasiveness, unusual in a man brought up from boyhood days in a wilderness land. The pages of Acadia&amp;rsquo;s history are much richer for his presence.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt; G&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;eorge &
2 CONC lt;/span&gt;M&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;ac&lt;/span&gt;B&lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;eath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;ACM, B.187, f.19. AE, M&amp;eacute;m. et doc., Am&amp;eacute;rique, 8. AN, Minutier, XV, 31; XX, 156; LXVI, 2
2 CONC 8. BN, &lt;span&gt;ms &lt;/span&gt;Fr.&amp;nbsp;18593, f.373; NAF 9281 (Margry), ff.69&amp;ndash;129. &amp;ldquo;Mass. Archives.&amp;rdquo; Champlain, &lt;em&gt;Works&lt;/em&gt; (Biggar). Charlevoix, &lt;em&gt;History&lt;/em&gt; (Shea). &lt;em&gt;C
2 CONC oll. de manuscrits relatifs &amp;agrave; la Nouv.-France&lt;/em&gt;, II, 351&amp;ndash;80 &lt;em&gt;passim&lt;/em&gt;.&amp;nbsp;Denys, &lt;em&gt;Description and natural history&lt;/em&gt; (Ganong). &lt;em&gt;Factum&lt;/em&gt; (1614). &lt;em&gt;M&am
2 CONC p;eacute;moires des commissaires&lt;/em&gt;, I, xiii, xxvi, xxxvii, 46&amp;ndash;48, 71, 80&amp;ndash;82, 84, 97, 106, 151; II, 276, 279&amp;ndash;80, 286&amp;ndash;89, 493, 511; IV, 86, 126, 162&amp;ndash;63, 180, 224ff., 232&amp;ndash;39, 241, 26
2 CONC 3, 305, 333, 443, 451, 499; and &lt;em&gt;Memorials of the English and French commissaries&lt;/em&gt;, I, 17, 41, 43, 118, 120, 145&amp;ndash;47, 155&amp;ndash;57, 168, 176, 206, 337, 361, 369, 488, 525, 570, 576&amp;ndash;79, 709, 727. PRO, &lt;em
2 CONC &gt;CSP&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Col., 1574&amp;ndash;1660&lt;/em&gt; [&lt;em&gt;see&lt;/em&gt; &amp;ldquo;Delatour&amp;rdquo;]; 1661&amp;ndash;68 [&lt;em&gt;see&lt;/em&gt; &amp;ldquo;St. Stephen&amp;rdquo;]; &lt;em&gt;1669&amp;ndash;74; 1697&amp;ndas
2 CONC h;98&lt;/em&gt; [&lt;em&gt;see&lt;/em&gt; &amp;ldquo;Delatour&amp;rdquo;]. &lt;em&gt;Suffolk deeds&lt;/em&gt;, I, 6&amp;ndash;10, 75&amp;ndash;76; III, 265, 268, 272, 276. &lt;em&gt;Winthrop&amp;rsquo;s journal&lt;/em&gt; (Hosmer) in &lt;em&gt;Orig
2 CONC inal narratives&lt;/em&gt; (Jameson). Ren&amp;eacute; Baudry, &amp;ldquo;Charles d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay et la Compagnie de la Nouvelle-France,&amp;rdquo; &lt;em&gt;RHAF&lt;/em&gt;, XI (1957&amp;ndash;58), 218&amp;ndash;41. J.&amp;nbsp;B.&amp;nbsp;Brebn
2 CONC er, &lt;em&gt;New England&amp;rsquo;s outpost: Acadia before the conquest of Canada&lt;/em&gt; (New York, 1927). Couillard Despr&amp;eacute;s, &lt;em&gt;Saint-&amp;Eacute;tienne de La&amp;nbsp;Tour&lt;/em&gt;, Huguet, &lt;em&gt;Poutrincourt,&lt;/em
2 CONC &gt; &amp;Eacute;mile Lauvri&amp;egrave;re, &lt;em&gt;Deux tra&amp;icirc;tres d&amp;rsquo;Acadie et leur victime: les Latour p&amp;egrave;re et fils et Charles d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay&lt;/em&gt; (Paris et Montr&amp;eacute;al, 1932). Edmund Slafter, &lt;
2 CONC em&gt;Sir&amp;nbsp;William Alexander and American colonization&lt;/em&gt; (Boston, 1873).&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;!--END BIBLIOGRAPHY--&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;General Bibliography&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;copy;&amp;nbsp;1966&amp;ndas
2 CONC h;2014&amp;nbsp;University of Toronto/Universit&amp;eacute; Laval&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
