1 _DSCR Origin Displayed: English - Origins Available: English, French. Spelling variations of this family name include: Bevis, Bevys, Beavis, Beavys and others. First found in Hampshire, where they were granted lands by King William after the Norman Conqu
2 CONC est in 1066 C.E. They were descended from the Beauvais in Normandy, and appear on the honor roll of Battell Abbey, as accompanying Duke William at Hastings in 1066 C.E. Some of the first settlers of this family name or some of its variants were: Be
2 CONC njamin Bevis who settled in Maryland in 1663; David Bevis settled in New England in 1698; Elizabeth Bevis settled in Virginia in 1654; Carol Bevois (Bevvis) settled in New Netherlands in 1659. (From www.HouseOfNames.com Archives copyright © 200
2 CONC 0 - 2009). This name, with variant spellings Be(a)ves, Be(e)vis(s), Beauvais and Bovis, has two possible origins - both French. The first is locational from Beauvais in Oise which was so called from the Gallic tribe who settled there, referred to i
2 CONC n Latin sources as the Bellovaci. Various other places in North France named from the late Latin "bellum visum", lovely sight, may also have given rise to the surname which first appears on record in England in the latter part of the 11th Century h
2 CONC aving been introduced by the Normans. Other early recordings include Simon de Beauveys (London, 1292), and Robert de Beueys, (Cambridgeshire, 1327). The second possibility is that the name derives from the Old French "bel fi(l)z", from "beu" or "be
2 CONC l", fair or lovely, plus "fi(l)z", a son, and originally given as an affectionate nickname to a favored person. Recordings include Odo Belfiz, (Hampshire, 1176) and William Beaufiz, (Gloucestershire, 1208). In 1614 one, Charles Beavis, Co. Devon, w
2 CONC as entered in "The Oxford University Register". The first recorded spelling of the family name is shown to be that of Goisbert de Beluaco, which was dated 1086, in the "The Domesday Book for Hertfordshire", during the reign of King William 1st, kno
2 CONC wn as "The Conqueror", 1066 - 1087. Surnames became necessary when governments introduced personal taxation. In England this was known as Poll Tax. Throughout the centuries, surnames in every country have continued to "develop" often leading to ast
2 CONC onishing variants of the original spelling. © Copyright: Name Origin Research www.surnamedb.com 1980 - 2015.
